It says it's a box.

6
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:10,000
Alright.

7
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:19,000
The two hours and forty minutes you've been waiting for since the last two hours and forty minutes will now begin.

8
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:25,000
Alright, so you may remember at the end of last class we were talking about system editing by ISTE Weeks of Economics,

9
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:28,000
which is a topic that probably got me into economics.

10
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:31,000
Because it wasn't learning about GDP that interested me.

11
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:35,000
It was about learning that everyone else was wrong that interested me.

12
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:43,000
Alright, I just wanted to know, yes, parents, teachers, media, all the people who have been telling you how society works are incorrect.

13
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:47,000
And not in some random way, but in the same way.

14
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:49,000
Like in a predictable way.

15
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:51,000
Like a systematic pattern.

16
00:00:51,000 --> 00:01:00,000
But this idea, while it is very prevalent in the teaching of a director of economics, is rarely found among economists as researchers.

17
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,000
Usually, especially when we're actually modeling economic beliefs, we propose that usual rational expectations assumption.

18
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:08,000
Right?

19
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:15,000
Which says that whatever people believe will be equal to truth plus some random error,

20
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:23,000
where the error is going to be random distributed with the mean zero and variance of sigma squared.

21
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:27,000
Alright, but anyway, notice, once you put it like that, it's testable.

22
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:31,000
And I worked a lot with this data set where it is in fact tested.

23
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:40,000
This is one where we got beliefs for both the general public and PhD economists specializing in domestic policy.

24
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:47,000
And we rely upon this implication of rational expectations, which is that if this is how error works,

25
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:51,000
then the difference between laymen and experts on average should be zero.

26
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:57,000
The only difference between the lay distribution of economic opinion, or any kind of opinion, and the expert one,

27
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:02,000
is that laymen should have a big error term and experts should have a small error term,

28
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:06,000
but they should both be centered around the true mean.

29
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:10,000
And that's where I get the annotation for the model.

30
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:21,000
So I've got measures of belief, and then progressives equal to a constant plus coefficient times a dummy variable for whether or not you're an economist.

31
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:25,000
And c is beta equal to zero.

32
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:31,000
And as I mentioned, it's almost never equal to zero, and in fact is often extremely far from zero.

33
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:33,000
You got the readings, we can go over that.

34
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:37,000
And furthermore, generally it's different from zero in the expected way.

35
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:43,000
Like the public is vastly more protectionist, and it has a protectionist view of the world.

36
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:48,000
So it's not simply that they have a different trade-off, they have a totally different picture about how the economy works,

37
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:55,000
which is roughly the kind of stupid model that Adam Smith made fun of in the 18th century, but probably stupider.

38
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:03,000
But we get one where the ideal world would be if we could sell tons of stuff to other countries and they never gave us anything back.

39
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:09,000
That would be a perfect world according to the typical non-economist view of international trade.

40
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:13,000
And again, according to the typical economist view of markets.

41
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:17,000
If there were no profits, this would be great for consumers.

42
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:20,000
According to the typical non-economist view of labor markets.

43
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:25,000
If no one was ever fired, that would be perfect functioning of labor markets.

44
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:30,000
And similarly, their view about how, say, the market for gasoline works.

45
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:33,000
Is it just a big OPEC setting the price?

46
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:38,000
And the countries you think aren't in OPEC, they're really in OPEC too.

47
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:44,000
And the key thing is that these views are not limited to just one political party, one faction.

48
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:48,000
These are by far the normal views, and there's a little bit of variation around them as we'll see.

49
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:56,000
But the main thing is just picture non-economists and economists as having two radically different views about how the economy works.

50
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:04,000
Now as I mentioned, there are of course people who do not trust economics and want to resolve the disagreement in favor of the public.

51
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:12,000
And say, look, yes, it's the so-called experts who have this delusional view of the world, and regular people see the world clearly.

52
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:20,000
Now all you're going to say is that, and it's very hard to show that it's wrong, just like if someone is accused of a crime,

53
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:24,000
and they get on the stand and they say, how do you explain that there's massive evidence against you?

54
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:26,000
And you say, I'm being framed.

55
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:29,000
All right, well, who's framing you? I don't know.

56
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:31,000
How are they framing you? I don't know that either.

57
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:33,000
Why are they framing you? Lord only knows.

58
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:37,000
All right, if that is a person's story, there's no way to disprove it.

59
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:43,000
However, does anyone ever get off when they're accused of a crime just by saying there is an indeterminate framing conspiracy?

60
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:45,000
Is this normal to get off? No.

61
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:51,000
Very, very rarely does someone get off on this because it's so ridiculous and it's too easy.

62
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:57,000
So normally if you want to go and say you're being framed, you need to give some specific story.

63
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:05,000
I'm being framed by Fred Smith, who has always hated me since childhood, and not coincidentally is the chief forensic investigator.

64
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:10,000
And he had 10 minutes alone with a camera mysteriously went off.

65
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:15,000
Huh? Huh? All right, now you are possibly going to win your case.

66
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:17,000
But you've got to give those kinds of details.

67
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:21,000
So similarly for economists, there are people who just say, I'm just a blanket distrust.

68
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:23,000
I don't have to say any more. No.

69
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:28,000
But normally people that want to tell the story will have a more specific, give you some details,

70
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:32,000
so they can flesh out the account.

71
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:35,000
There's a line in Gilbert and Sullivan where they say, well,

72
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:40,000
to lend an ear of verisimilitude to an otherwise bald and unconvincing narrative.

73
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:45,000
All right, that's what they were, to lend an ear of verisimilitude to an otherwise bald and unconvincing narrative.

74
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:52,000
And that's where you bring in the alternative explanations of what's going on to say why the economists are so deviant.

75
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:56,000
And one story is self-summary bias, which says that economists are rich for 10 years.

76
00:05:56,000 --> 00:05:59,000
So, of course, they believe this nonsense.

77
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:04,000
In other words, they imagine that whatever is good for them is good for society.

78
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:09,000
And then another story that is also told is ideological bias.

79
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:14,000
It just says, look, economics is a coven of right wing ideology.

80
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:18,000
So, you know, democracy and change, that's the whole economics profession.

81
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:23,000
James Buchanan, Bill Friedman, they are the economists who define the whole profession.

82
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:32,000
And so naturally, you wouldn't go into these fields unless you wanted to be part of the conspiracy of democracy and change and give all power to the king of shame.

83
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:35,000
So that is another story.

84
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:38,000
All right, so these are two main stories that have been given.

85
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:40,000
And notice they are both tested.

86
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:47,000
They are in principle testable, because if this is your story, then it should be that if you add on a vector of controls,

87
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:56,000
so instead of saying self-serving bias, so again, like income, job security, as many other things as you might think are relevant,

88
00:06:56,000 --> 00:07:02,000
and then you could add on some more controls for different measures of politics.

89
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:09,000
It says that once you add on all these control variables, then E will go down to zero.

90
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:13,000
And the actual result is this is totally wrong.

91
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:17,000
Self-serving bias is at most a small part of the story.

92
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:23,000
So if you look at the absolute value of the coefficients, it falls by about 18%.

93
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:28,000
Oftentimes, the coefficient doesn't even fit with self-serving bias at all.

94
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:32,000
Like economists are less concerned about the economic damage of welfare than the general public.

95
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:34,000
How many economists go on welfare?

96
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:36,000
How many think they're likely to go on welfare?

97
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:43,000
So it seems like self-serving bias should lead them to exaggerate, but we don't see that in many similar cases.

98
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:47,000
So anyway, self-serving bias at most is a small part of the story,

99
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:54,000
and ideological bias is a negative part of the story, because the typical economist is not right-wing at all.

100
00:07:54,000 --> 00:08:02,000
The typical economist is a moderate Democrat, or like a center-left Democrat is where the typical economist lines up,

101
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:06,000
which, if you remember the way that regressions work, makes it all the more surprising.

102
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:11,000
So if a center-left Democrat still says that supply and demand runs the price of gasoline,

103
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:15,000
this is a case where someone you would ideologically expect would not say that says it anyway,

104
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:21,000
and therefore the effect of economic training seems to be even larger than it appears.

105
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:25,000
So if, in other words, you come from a demographic where the non-economists,

106
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:29,000
so 90% of them would say it's collusion, only 10% would say it's supply and demand,

107
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:40,000
if 80% of the center-left economists say that it's supply and demand, and only 20% say that it's collusion,

108
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:46,000
then this enormous gap is going to get attributed not to ideology, right, because the ideology is the same,

109
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:49,000
it's going to get attributed to the economic training.

110
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:59,000
So, anyway, so this story, almost totally wrong, this story more than completely wrong, right?

111
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:03,000
When you adjust for that, the gaps actually get bigger.

112
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:07,000
Then, if you were to go and summarize the clusters of errors,

113
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:12,000
four big generalizations, one of them is anti-market bias.

114
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:18,000
So compared to economists, non-economists tend to underestimate the social benefits of markets.

115
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:23,000
This does not mean, of course, that the typical economist is a flaming libertarian,

116
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:30,000
it just means that there is a big tendency towards favoring markets for economists compared to the general public.

117
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:36,000
And it's so strong that many center-left economists just have forgotten what normal people are like.

118
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:41,000
So there they're staking out their boldly interventionist position relative to other economists,

119
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:48,000
forgetting that by the standards of any normal person, they are a free market ideologue.

120
00:09:48,000 --> 00:09:54,000
So, again, this is very apparent in things like belief that supply and demand controls the market for gasoline,

121
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:59,000
where even among very conservative Republicans, if you're a non-economist,

122
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:04,000
you still, still only about 30% of you believe, of them would believe in supply and demand running things.

123
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:11,000
So, again, they are more likely to embrace the supply and demand story, but still highly unlikely.

124
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:16,000
So, again, I always think about my dad, who's, anytime you mention new entry,

125
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:21,000
it is very quick to explain to you the new entry will do no good for customers ever,

126
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:25,000
because when a new office depot opens up, what does my dad say,

127
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:28,000
stables and office depot have already done?

128
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:33,000
They've already gotten in the back room, lit up the cigars, and agreed that the prices will never go down.

129
00:10:33,000 --> 00:10:39,000
And this is true for every industry in the world, except for the one my dad has any first-hand experience with,

130
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:41,000
which is defense contracting.

131
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:42,000
Alright.

132
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:46,000
It's the one he saw with his own eyes, where he experienced that as ultra-competitive.

133
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:52,000
My inclination to say that what my dad saw with his own eyes is probably basically true.

134
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:57,000
And when we hear about stories of collusion and defense contracting, even there is the exception,

135
00:10:57,000 --> 00:11:02,000
and the normal state of affairs is people desperately trying to get some business.

136
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:03,000
Alright.

137
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:09,000
So we've got the anti-market bias, anti-foreign bias, getting especially pessimistic when foreigners are concerned,

138
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:13,000
especially likely to think that foreigners are just exploiting us and ripping us off.

139
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:20,000
Then make-work bias, to evaluating the performance of the economy by employment rather than production, right,

140
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:24,000
which is always tempting at any one point in time, and yet, if you think about what would happen

141
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:31,000
if we just tried to save all jobs, it would freeze the economy into a state of total hypnosis,

142
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:33,000
or not hypnosis, self-stagnation.

143
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:35,000
Yes, yes. What's the other word?

144
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:36,000
Hossification.

145
00:11:36,000 --> 00:11:38,000
Yes, total state of total hossification.

146
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:40,000
It's the other OS word.

147
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:41,000
Alright.

148
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:43,000
And then finally, pessimistic bias.

149
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:47,000
Compared to economists, the general public believes in doom and gloom,

150
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:52,000
the economy's in decline, is it bad now, is it going to get even worse?

151
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:58,000
And by the way, there is one question that allows us to check the hypothesis

152
00:11:58,000 --> 00:12:02,000
that economists just have a longer view of the world, so it's not a true difference of disagreement

153
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:06,000
about facts, it's a disagreement about what is the time horizon you care about.

154
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:10,000
So this one question that I was asked in 1996, this specifically said,

155
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:16,000
20 years from now, what do you think the effect of downsizing increases international competitiveness,

156
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:22,000
and let's see, so downsizing international competitiveness, and someone, what was the other one?

157
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:26,000
Oh yeah, I'm improving on that one, we can enhance technology, we'll be on the U.S. economy.

158
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:34,000
And so anyway, so notice, it's 1996, 20 years later, so the time frame of the question has already occurred,

159
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:40,000
two years ago, and so 20 years have passed, and this is one where, in terms of the absolute gap

160
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:43,000
between economists and the public, it may even be bigger than the other gaps,

161
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:48,000
at least it is the same in the same general ballpark, where economists, when you ask about long-run effects,

162
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:52,000
almost 100% say good, the effects would be good for the economy.

163
00:12:52,000 --> 00:12:56,000
Whereas the public, the average answer is neutral.

164
00:12:56,000 --> 00:13:01,000
So basically for the public, those three things I mentioned, improved technology, increased competitiveness,

165
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:08,000
downsizing, are like a hurricane. Terrible initially, 20 years later, the effect's gone,

166
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:11,000
but it sounds like there was some good coming out of it? No.

167
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:15,000
In fact, probably the public's more willing to think that hurricanes have good effects on the economy,

168
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:19,000
whenever there's a hurricane and people start talking about it as good for the glass makers,

169
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:26,000
only they're a Frenchman from the 1840s, who had said something about this possible fallacy.

170
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:34,000
All right, so there we are. So questions about any of these general patterns? Yes?

171
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:39,000
Yeah, I was wondering, is there any evidence that you have the same sort of public bias

172
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:42,000
in other social science fields, like sociology, psychology?

173
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:46,000
Ah, good question. So I have a paper on political science, where I,

174
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:50,000
and particularly this is on who in government is responsible for which outcomes.

175
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:56,000
And so this one is the only time I ever created a new data set, which I don't recommend doing,

176
00:13:56,000 --> 00:13:58,000
unless you have any idea where to avoid it.

177
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:02,000
But, yeah, what I found there is that there were significant differences,

178
00:14:02,000 --> 00:14:08,000
not as big as on economics, but again, things like the general public thinks

179
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:10,000
that the Federal Reserve controls the budget.

180
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:14,000
And they think the Supreme Court has a big effect on the crime rate,

181
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:18,000
whereas actual political scientists don't think the Supreme Court has much effect on the crime rate.

182
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:22,000
So, yeah, there are definitely gaps there.

183
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:27,000
And let's see, so actually in Myth of a Rational Voter, I talked about this paper on toxicology,

184
00:14:27,000 --> 00:14:33,000
where, yes, compared to professional toxicologists, the general public is very inclined to the view

185
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:38,000
that dosage of poisons does not matter, and the slightest dose of a dangerous thing will kill you for sure.

186
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:43,000
Very popular view among the public, for example.

187
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:44,000
Let's see.

188
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:48,000
And then, if you take my Graduate Public Choice class, go over some other ones,

189
00:14:48,000 --> 00:14:53,000
in foreign policy there's a very well-documented thing called the rally around the flag effect,

190
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:58,000
which says that you can make a war popular simply by declaring it,

191
00:14:58,000 --> 00:15:03,000
and here's the kicker, support, once you, after you get that spike,

192
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:06,000
support predictably erodes regardless of what occurs.

193
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:10,000
So it doesn't seem that it really matters whether the war is successful or not,

194
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:16,000
it's just that initially people get excited and then they become disappointed.

195
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:21,000
Which, in terms of any kind of rational inference, it's hard to explain,

196
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:24,000
in terms of a totally emotional story, it makes a lot of sense, right?

197
00:15:24,000 --> 00:15:30,000
So, you have a friend who falls in love on a regular basis, and every time he falls in love,

198
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:35,000
and he says, this is the one, and then very predictably, ten weeks later, this person's terrible.

199
00:15:35,000 --> 00:15:40,000
Right, if that happens, spike, down, spike, down, spike, down, if that happens ten times in a row,

200
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:45,000
it's like, hmm, doesn't seem to be a rational inference here, it seems more like you have some strong emotions,

201
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:51,000
and then you treat those as facts, as somehow like an access to facts, but they're not an access to facts,

202
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:56,000
they are a reason that you ignore facts, which is the fact that things never work out for you.

203
00:15:56,000 --> 00:15:57,000
Sorry.

204
00:15:57,000 --> 00:16:05,000
Yes, follow up?

205
00:16:05,000 --> 00:16:09,000
Is there any overlap between the biases in the examples you're giving?

206
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:16,000
Let's see, so, almost certainly, so again, just to be clear, these clusters are not identified statistically,

207
00:16:16,000 --> 00:16:23,000
they are identified just by eyeballing it, seeing what are some patterns that are there.

208
00:16:23,000 --> 00:16:31,000
So, I couldn't think of any other way, although people see, people like the eyeball summaries better than the actual statistics in practice,

209
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:32,000
and that's what people remember.

