240
00:49:41,580 --> 00:49:45,060
earnings, five years worth of whatever other costs there are.

241
00:49:45,060 --> 00:49:48,340
So that's going to mess things up.

242
00:49:48,340 --> 00:49:50,500
But then for college, then it's really bleak.

243
00:49:50,500 --> 00:49:53,860
So this is full-time undergraduates,

244
00:49:53,860 --> 00:49:56,260
full-time people in four-year programs.

245
00:49:56,260 --> 00:49:59,620
So about 40% finish in four years.

246
00:49:59,620 --> 00:50:02,700
So a majority of undergraduate student

247
00:50:02,700 --> 00:50:04,740
who are studying full-time, remember,

248
00:50:04,740 --> 00:50:06,660
we're not talking about the part-timers,

249
00:50:06,660 --> 00:50:08,820
we're not talking about people who are semi-committed.

250
00:50:08,820 --> 00:50:11,700
We're talking about people who, according to their parents,

251
00:50:11,700 --> 00:50:13,620
are full-time college students that are paying full-time

252
00:50:13,620 --> 00:50:15,820
tuition and are probably not working.

253
00:50:15,820 --> 00:50:16,780
I put in all of them.

254
00:50:16,780 --> 00:50:17,940
Look at them.

255
00:50:17,940 --> 00:50:21,380
And only under 40% of them are actually finishing

256
00:50:21,380 --> 00:50:22,900
within four years.

257
00:50:22,900 --> 00:50:25,980
How about finishing a four-year degree with five full-time

258
00:50:25,980 --> 00:50:27,020
years?

259
00:50:27,020 --> 00:50:28,420
55%.

260
00:50:28,420 --> 00:50:30,540
Six, like 60%.

261
00:50:30,540 --> 00:50:35,460
Now, that number may be a little bit low because there's

262
00:50:35,460 --> 00:50:37,860
very few of them because there are people transferring,

263
00:50:37,860 --> 00:50:40,220
and a lot of data sets mess up the transfers.

264
00:50:40,220 --> 00:50:42,740
So a reasonable rule of thumb is maybe

265
00:50:42,740 --> 00:50:45,500
you want to raise those numbers up by 10%, not 10 percentage

266
00:50:45,500 --> 00:50:48,460
points, but raise them up by 10% to factor in the transfers.

267
00:50:48,460 --> 00:50:50,140
Because also, of course, a lot of times,

268
00:50:50,140 --> 00:50:53,460
transfers lead to taking extra years because somebody's credit

269
00:50:53,460 --> 00:50:54,260
just transferred.

270
00:50:54,260 --> 00:50:55,620
Is that one?

271
00:50:55,620 --> 00:50:56,340
All right.

272
00:50:56,340 --> 00:50:59,740
So the completion probability, a crucial thing to put in.

273
00:50:59,740 --> 00:51:01,380
And it's even more important than it

274
00:51:01,380 --> 00:51:08,260
seems on the surface because of the dreaded sheepskin effect.

275
00:51:08,260 --> 00:51:10,660
A very strong empirical regularity education

276
00:51:10,660 --> 00:51:12,420
that says that most of the payoff,

277
00:51:12,420 --> 00:51:14,460
at least a disproportionate amount of the payoff,

278
00:51:14,460 --> 00:51:16,660
comes from graduation year.

279
00:51:16,660 --> 00:51:18,180
Earlier years don't pay very much.

280
00:51:18,180 --> 00:51:20,340
It's graduation year that really matters.

281
00:51:20,340 --> 00:51:24,260
So in my book, I do a full literature search.

282
00:51:24,260 --> 00:51:26,340
I come up, for high school, the senior year

283
00:51:26,340 --> 00:51:29,740
pays a bit more than the first three years combined.

284
00:51:29,740 --> 00:51:31,500
For undergraduate, the senior year

285
00:51:31,500 --> 00:51:34,260
pays more than double the first three years combined.

286
00:51:34,260 --> 00:51:35,740
And that's all in percentage terms,

287
00:51:35,740 --> 00:51:39,900
so it's even more lopsided than it sounds.

288
00:51:39,900 --> 00:51:43,620
So what this means is that if you don't finish,

289
00:51:43,620 --> 00:51:47,500
college saying, which is highly likely that you won't finish.

290
00:51:47,500 --> 00:51:50,580
You don't finish, then you are losing almost everything.

291
00:51:50,580 --> 00:51:53,100
You're getting almost nothing financially out of it

292
00:51:53,100 --> 00:51:54,940
because so much of the payoff comes

293
00:51:54,940 --> 00:51:56,860
from crossing the finish line.

294
00:51:56,860 --> 00:51:58,780
Now, notice this here is tacitly assuming

295
00:51:58,780 --> 00:52:00,300
there is no sheepskin effect.

296
00:52:00,300 --> 00:52:02,380
So again, the obvious thing is to redo this

297
00:52:02,380 --> 00:52:04,220
where we've got education years, and then we

298
00:52:04,220 --> 00:52:06,060
have dummy variables from the degrees.

299
00:52:06,060 --> 00:52:09,380
And then again, you'll notice, ah, this is actually

300
00:52:09,380 --> 00:52:11,820
a quite small payoff for years.

301
00:52:11,820 --> 00:52:15,420
It's the degrees that really count.

302
00:52:15,420 --> 00:52:17,220
So this is a huge problem.

303
00:52:17,220 --> 00:52:21,260
And again, if you just go and do some very simple math,

304
00:52:21,260 --> 00:52:23,180
it's pretty obvious what's going on.

305
00:52:23,180 --> 00:52:26,300
So again, suppose that you are lending out money.

306
00:52:26,300 --> 00:52:29,580
All the loans are 10% interest, but suppose that half

307
00:52:29,580 --> 00:52:32,540
don't come back at all.

308
00:52:32,540 --> 00:52:34,700
So suppose half the time, the person

309
00:52:34,700 --> 00:52:37,140
just absconds with the money and you get nothing back.

310
00:52:37,140 --> 00:52:40,260
Then what kind of a profit rate do you actually have?

311
00:52:40,260 --> 00:52:46,540
So 0.5 times 1.1 plus 0.5 times 0.

312
00:52:46,540 --> 00:52:48,700
So imagine it's a 50% repayment rate.

313
00:52:48,700 --> 00:52:54,740
In that case, is your business model even sustainable?

314
00:52:54,740 --> 00:52:56,180
No way.

315
00:52:56,180 --> 00:53:00,700
So what you've got is a rate of return of negative 45%

316
00:53:00,700 --> 00:53:02,420
on your investment.

317
00:53:02,420 --> 00:53:04,340
So you suppose there are no rates.

318
00:53:04,340 --> 00:53:06,020
So that can be like, fine, that's crazy.

319
00:53:06,020 --> 00:53:09,420
Or what if we have 95% repayments

320
00:53:09,420 --> 00:53:12,780
and 5% absconding?

321
00:53:12,780 --> 00:53:13,380
You went there.

322
00:53:13,380 --> 00:53:14,780
Now we're talking.

323
00:53:14,780 --> 00:53:19,580
So a 5% rate of default brings your expected rate

324
00:53:19,580 --> 00:53:21,820
of return down from about 10 to 5.

325
00:53:21,820 --> 00:53:24,460
So it halves the rate.

326
00:53:24,460 --> 00:53:27,500
So again, these defaults are not a marginal issue.

327
00:53:27,500 --> 00:53:30,380
They are the prime issue from the point of view

328
00:53:30,380 --> 00:53:33,580
of the individual investment.

329
00:53:33,580 --> 00:53:36,020
This is such an extreme result. I remember years ago,

330
00:53:36,020 --> 00:53:37,740
I was arguing with Alex Tavarock.

331
00:53:37,740 --> 00:53:39,540
And when I told him this, he just said,

332
00:53:39,540 --> 00:53:41,220
legal labor economists cannot be making

333
00:53:41,220 --> 00:53:44,380
such a ridiculous, stupid, blatant error.

334
00:53:44,380 --> 00:53:46,740
And I said, oh, yes, they are.

335
00:53:46,740 --> 00:53:49,420
And he said, oh, come on, come on.

336
00:53:49,420 --> 00:53:53,820
So we agreed to an arbiter, a well-known and mutually

337
00:53:53,820 --> 00:53:56,060
trusted labor economist, Gordon Dahl.

338
00:53:56,060 --> 00:53:58,020
And so we collectively emailed him.

339
00:53:58,020 --> 00:53:59,420
Gordon, we have a dispute.

340
00:53:59,420 --> 00:54:01,540
I'm not going to tell you which side either is on.

341
00:54:01,540 --> 00:54:03,420
But here's a dispute.

342
00:54:03,420 --> 00:54:07,260
Is it true that a standard return to education estimates

343
00:54:07,260 --> 00:54:09,140
ignore completion probability?

344
00:54:09,140 --> 00:54:10,780
He wrote back, yes.

345
00:54:10,780 --> 00:54:12,420
Yes, it is true.

346
00:54:12,420 --> 00:54:15,020
And Alex was like, what?

347
00:54:15,020 --> 00:54:16,420
Alex was awesome.

348
00:54:16,420 --> 00:54:18,100
And he's one of the very few people

349
00:54:18,100 --> 00:54:19,940
I've ever met who gave me odds, because he

350
00:54:19,940 --> 00:54:21,100
said he was very confident.

351
00:54:21,100 --> 00:54:22,820
So he gave me 2 to 1, paid right off.

352
00:54:26,940 --> 00:54:29,020
So in the last five years, there have

353
00:54:29,020 --> 00:54:32,180
been a few papers on the subject getting published.

354
00:54:32,180 --> 00:54:33,700
Before that, there's one by Atanji,

355
00:54:33,700 --> 00:54:35,540
super famous economist in the early 90s,

356
00:54:35,540 --> 00:54:37,380
and he pointed this out.

357
00:54:37,380 --> 00:54:38,820
Now, you may say, what do you mean?

358
00:54:38,820 --> 00:54:40,860
There's lots of stuff on completion probability.

359
00:54:40,860 --> 00:54:42,860
Now, there's lots of stuff on the determinants

360
00:54:42,860 --> 00:54:44,620
of completion probability.

361
00:54:44,620 --> 00:54:46,500
There, there's tons of papers on that.

362
00:54:46,500 --> 00:54:49,260
How can we get completion probability up?

363
00:54:49,260 --> 00:54:50,980
What is completion probability?

364
00:54:50,980 --> 00:54:52,620
But there's almost no papers that

365
00:54:52,620 --> 00:54:54,380
go and take the completion probability

366
00:54:54,380 --> 00:54:58,900
and put it back into the return calculation.

367
00:54:58,900 --> 00:55:01,100
And if you think that labor economists and education

368
00:55:01,100 --> 00:55:05,540
economists have a pro-education bias, as I do,

369
00:55:05,540 --> 00:55:08,500
I think this is the strongest possible way.

370
00:55:08,500 --> 00:55:10,740
If you think this, it's super suspicious.

371
00:55:10,740 --> 00:55:13,100
It's like, well, but the reason you're trying to raise it

372
00:55:13,100 --> 00:55:16,500
is to get the return up, right?

373
00:55:16,500 --> 00:55:20,020
And so shouldn't we go and talk about how it's

374
00:55:20,020 --> 00:55:22,700
affecting the return right now?

375
00:55:22,700 --> 00:55:25,300
And there's some sense that's defeatist to go and say, well,

376
00:55:25,300 --> 00:55:27,140
what if we're going to go and put in the fact

377
00:55:27,140 --> 00:55:28,380
that half of them fail, then it's

378
00:55:28,380 --> 00:55:30,800
a good ingredient, why am I saying almost none of them

379
00:55:30,800 --> 00:55:31,300
should go?

380
00:55:31,300 --> 00:55:33,580
All right, well, isn't that true?

381
00:55:33,580 --> 00:55:36,300
Until you figure out a way to get that completion probability

382
00:55:36,300 --> 00:55:39,980
way up, isn't it true that it's a bad idea?

383
00:55:39,980 --> 00:55:41,500
And again, this is the kind of thing

384
00:55:41,500 --> 00:55:43,980
where economists have so often mocked people

385
00:55:43,980 --> 00:55:46,580
who open up restaurants ignoring the fact that 80% of them

386
00:55:46,580 --> 00:55:49,220
fail the first year.

387
00:55:49,220 --> 00:55:51,260
And some are going to say, well, but what if you

388
00:55:51,260 --> 00:55:52,540
had a really good idea?

389
00:55:52,540 --> 00:55:54,460
All right, yeah, well, if you had a good idea,

390
00:55:54,460 --> 00:55:54,940
that would change things.

391
00:55:54,940 --> 00:55:56,180
But you probably don't have a good idea.

392
00:55:56,180 --> 00:55:57,860
Why do you think you have a good idea?

393
00:55:57,860 --> 00:56:00,220
Isn't that what almost everybody thinks?

394
00:56:00,220 --> 00:56:04,660
So you need to have some sign on top of your own O of confidence,

395
00:56:04,660 --> 00:56:09,060
which almost everybody at an open restaurant has.

396
00:56:09,060 --> 00:56:12,820
So obviously, if you were giving practical advice to someone

397
00:56:12,820 --> 00:56:14,500
thinking of opening a business, you

398
00:56:14,500 --> 00:56:16,660
should totally factor in the odds

399
00:56:16,660 --> 00:56:20,340
that they lose their life savings, right?

400
00:56:20,340 --> 00:56:22,500
If you know someone who's going to open a business,

401
00:56:22,500 --> 00:56:25,340
will you not reach out to them and try to help them?

402
00:56:25,340 --> 00:56:27,060
Will you not use the power of economics

403
00:56:27,060 --> 00:56:28,980
to save your friend's life savings,

404
00:56:28,980 --> 00:56:31,180
or at least let them be warned?

405
00:56:31,180 --> 00:56:34,460
By the way, you realize what the failure rate is here.

406
00:56:34,460 --> 00:56:36,580
And you might say, maybe you should try a business

407
00:56:36,580 --> 00:56:37,660
with a lower failure rate.

408
00:56:37,660 --> 00:56:39,040
Maybe you want to build a business

409
00:56:39,040 --> 00:56:40,460
for a little lower failure rates.

410
00:56:40,460 --> 00:56:42,860
Maybe you should try something like that.

411
00:56:42,860 --> 00:56:48,340
So this is an enormous factor, which is so neglected,

412
00:56:48,340 --> 00:56:52,620
it's almost mind-boggling, but there it is.

413
00:56:52,620 --> 00:56:54,540
And as I mentioned, it's more mind-boggling

414
00:56:54,540 --> 00:56:56,340
when you interact with the Sheepskin Effect

415
00:56:56,340 --> 00:56:58,980
and realize that doing three years of school

416
00:56:58,980 --> 00:57:04,260
and then giving up has almost no payoff, almost no gross payoff.

417
00:57:04,260 --> 00:57:05,860
So if you have almost no gross payoff

418
00:57:05,860 --> 00:57:08,620
and you have three years of net costs,

419
00:57:08,620 --> 00:57:12,700
then you are really in trouble.

420
00:57:12,700 --> 00:57:15,140
Questions about that?

421
00:57:15,140 --> 00:57:19,940
And now we come to a last horrible problem, which

422
00:57:19,940 --> 00:57:27,220
is that this result does not control for preexisting

423
00:57:27,220 --> 00:57:27,820
ability.

424
00:57:27,820 --> 00:57:29,780
And there's at least one preexisting ability

425
00:57:29,780 --> 00:57:32,900
where we have very strong evidence that it's important.

426
00:57:32,900 --> 00:57:34,500
And that is intelligence.

427
00:57:40,140 --> 00:57:42,660
And intelligence is known to be highly correlated

428
00:57:42,660 --> 00:57:47,980
with education with correlation of 0.6, 0.7.

429
00:57:47,980 --> 00:57:48,820
Yes?

430
00:57:48,820 --> 00:57:50,580
So you said that labor terms don't

431
00:57:50,580 --> 00:57:52,100
have any prediction effect?

432
00:57:52,100 --> 00:57:52,600
Yes.

433
00:57:52,600 --> 00:57:54,380
So they also are not taking into account

434
00:57:54,380 --> 00:57:56,100
what Sheepskin Effect does?

435
00:57:56,100 --> 00:57:59,860
So yes, the Sheepskin Effect, while agreed to by virtually

436
00:57:59,860 --> 00:58:01,420
everyone who's ever looked at the data,

437
00:58:01,420 --> 00:58:03,660
is very rarely, very rarely actually referenced

438
00:58:03,660 --> 00:58:04,380
in most articles.

439
00:58:04,380 --> 00:58:06,140
Most articles just like to do it this way.

440
00:58:08,460 --> 00:58:11,820
So that is the strange thing is the effect

441
00:58:11,820 --> 00:58:15,220
is so large and so consistent in virtually every paper I've ever

442
00:58:15,220 --> 00:58:17,900
found in the last 25 years.

443
00:58:17,900 --> 00:58:19,820
See, when I was debating Eric Haineshek,

444
00:58:19,820 --> 00:58:21,420
he did throw me for a curve by saying,

445
00:58:21,420 --> 00:58:23,020
well, I've got a data set where there's

446
00:58:23,020 --> 00:58:24,300
a much of a Sheepskin Effect.

447
00:58:24,300 --> 00:58:26,980
And so anyway, so at least for college,

448
00:58:26,980 --> 00:58:29,140
his data set doesn't actually have years of college.

449
00:58:29,140 --> 00:58:32,060
So his results aren't really comparable.

450
00:58:32,060 --> 00:58:33,940
For high school, his results are just

451
00:58:33,940 --> 00:58:35,740
at the low end of what other data sets say.

452
00:58:35,740 --> 00:58:37,220
So it's not really that disturbing.

453
00:58:37,220 --> 00:58:39,260
But when you're in a debate with someone,

454
00:58:39,260 --> 00:58:40,820
he says, I got some regressions here.

455
00:58:40,820 --> 00:58:41,940
And they say, you're wrong.

456
00:58:45,060 --> 00:58:47,780
Because I can't honestly say too much about it,

457
00:58:47,780 --> 00:58:50,180
other than it goes against everything and everybody else.

458
00:58:50,180 --> 00:58:54,540
But I didn't want to accuse him of making a statistical error.

459
00:58:54,540 --> 00:58:55,900
It seems super unlikely.

460
00:58:59,300 --> 00:59:01,380
So anyway, you may have noticed that people

461
00:59:01,380 --> 00:59:04,540
that get more education not only are smarter at the end,

462
00:59:04,540 --> 00:59:06,340
but were smarter at the beginning.

463
00:59:06,340 --> 00:59:08,420
Because of course, if you were smarter at the end,

464
00:59:08,420 --> 00:59:10,580
you might say, well, that's caused by the education.

465
00:59:10,580 --> 00:59:13,300
But this is where you get an early measure of intelligence,

466
00:59:13,300 --> 00:59:15,000
or at least when preceding the education,

467
00:59:15,000 --> 00:59:17,580
use it to predict educational attainment.

468
00:59:17,580 --> 00:59:22,260
And this is highly effective.

469
00:59:22,260 --> 00:59:27,180
So now let us do a regression on intelligence.

470
00:59:27,180 --> 00:59:29,540
First of all, say we all have an intuitive notion of what

471
00:59:29,540 --> 00:59:30,900
it means to be intelligent.

472
00:59:30,900 --> 00:59:33,580
So if you describe another human being as intelligent,

473
00:59:33,580 --> 00:59:36,220
most people don't say, well, what does that mean?

474
00:59:36,220 --> 00:59:37,220
What would that be like?

475
00:59:37,220 --> 00:59:38,740
I don't understand the word.

476
00:59:38,740 --> 00:59:40,980
The word makes no sense to me.

477
00:59:40,980 --> 00:59:42,740
So instead, normally, you know just

478
00:59:42,740 --> 00:59:45,260
what they're talking about, or at least in general terms,

479
00:59:45,260 --> 00:59:46,760
you know what they're talking about.

480
00:59:46,760 --> 00:59:49,060
So you have a basic ballpark idea.

481
00:59:49,060 --> 00:59:50,680
You might ask some follow-up questions,

482
00:59:50,680 --> 00:59:53,140
like are they like a kind of intelligent person

483
00:59:53,140 --> 00:59:55,380
who's really good at math, but nothing else?

484
00:59:55,380 --> 00:59:58,660
Or are they good at lots of things?

485
00:59:58,660 --> 01:00:18,100
And on the other hand, you all have an idea.


1
01:00:00,000 --> 01:00:02,160
about things that would not generally count against your intelligence.

2
01:00:02,160 --> 01:00:05,960
Like we found that Albert Einstein was really bad at dancing.

3
01:00:05,960 --> 01:00:10,440
Would this make you reassess your view of Albert's intelligence?

4
01:00:10,440 --> 01:00:14,320
Now probably you would reassess your view of what?

5
01:00:14,320 --> 01:00:17,240
His dexterity, his coordination.

6
01:00:17,240 --> 01:00:19,560
You might even reassess his common sense, right?

7
01:00:19,560 --> 01:00:21,640
Because a person who is bad at dancing

8
01:00:21,640 --> 01:00:24,000
with common sense would simply not dance, right?

9
01:00:24,000 --> 01:00:26,680
Or at least avoid him.

10
01:00:26,680 --> 01:00:28,720
Depending on the situation, of course.

11
01:00:28,720 --> 01:00:30,680
You might just say, I don't need the people, I enjoy it.

12
01:00:30,680 --> 01:00:33,520
So who cares about that?

13
01:00:33,520 --> 01:00:33,920
All right.

14
01:00:33,920 --> 01:00:36,080
Well, anyway, there's a huge empirical literature

15
01:00:36,080 --> 01:00:40,520
on intelligence in psychology.

16
01:00:40,520 --> 01:00:42,520
There, you know, like people sometimes have an idea

17
01:00:42,520 --> 01:00:43,680
that this only used to happen.

18
01:00:43,680 --> 01:00:45,240
It's ongoing.

19
01:00:45,240 --> 01:00:46,240
They do enormous amount.

20
01:00:46,240 --> 01:00:48,880
They don't publicize it that much because people get mad

21
01:00:48,880 --> 01:00:49,920
at them for publicizing it.

22
01:00:49,920 --> 01:00:51,880
So they tend to keep it a little bit close to the chest.

23
01:00:51,880 --> 01:00:55,880
But in terms of publications, there's a whole lot going on.

24
01:00:55,880 --> 01:00:58,440
And unending.

25
01:00:58,440 --> 01:00:59,000
All right.

26
01:00:59,000 --> 01:01:01,560
So in practical terms, researchers usually measure it

27
01:01:01,560 --> 01:01:04,760
with tests that are called IQ tests.

28
01:01:04,760 --> 01:01:07,400
Like, so it's for intelligence quotients.

29
01:01:07,400 --> 01:01:08,560
Or related tests.

30
01:01:08,560 --> 01:01:10,480
Actually, so it's been a very long time

31
01:01:10,480 --> 01:01:12,400
since anyone has actually handed out a test

32
01:01:12,400 --> 01:01:15,280
with the letters I and Q on it.

33
01:01:15,280 --> 01:01:16,480
Normally, there's other tests.

34
01:01:16,480 --> 01:01:20,160
But anyone ever taken like, you know, the Wechsler or the Weiss

35
01:01:20,160 --> 01:01:21,440
or the Wunderlich?

36
01:01:21,440 --> 01:01:23,800
Or actually, if you've done the SAT or the GRE,

37
01:01:23,800 --> 01:01:26,440
those are, for all practical purposes,

38
01:01:26,440 --> 01:01:29,960
just other intelligence tests.

39
01:01:29,960 --> 01:01:32,280
There is a big effort by the Educational Testing Service

40
01:01:32,280 --> 01:01:32,960
to deny this.

41
01:01:32,960 --> 01:01:35,040
I don't know that they've ever actually sued anyone

42
01:01:35,040 --> 01:01:35,880
for saying this.

43
01:01:35,880 --> 01:01:38,080
So I'm glad you showed them a start now.

44
01:01:38,080 --> 01:01:42,960
But you can even see it in the publicity.

45
01:01:42,960 --> 01:01:49,120
So back in the 1980s, what did SAT stand for?

46
01:01:49,120 --> 01:01:51,440
Scholastic Aptitude Test.

47
01:01:51,440 --> 01:01:54,680
Now, what does it even mean to say someone's highest

48
01:01:54,680 --> 01:01:57,360
scholastic aptitude but low in intelligence?

49
01:01:57,360 --> 01:01:59,280
Can you think of what that would be?

50
01:01:59,280 --> 01:02:01,160
They seem almost synonymous.

51
01:02:01,160 --> 01:02:02,760
But anyway, in the 90s, they decided,

52
01:02:02,760 --> 01:02:04,360
eh, we don't want to call that anymore.

53
01:02:04,360 --> 01:02:05,680
It gets people mad.

54
01:02:05,680 --> 01:02:07,240
But we don't want to change the name.

55
01:02:07,240 --> 01:02:08,480
We have a name brand.

56
01:02:08,480 --> 01:02:09,980
So then they said, all right, fine.

57
01:02:09,980 --> 01:02:15,680
Now, SAT stands for Scholastic Achievement Test.

58
01:02:15,680 --> 01:02:18,160
So now, it doesn't measure aptitude for anything.

59
01:02:18,160 --> 01:02:19,400
It measures your achievements.

60
01:02:19,400 --> 01:02:22,040
It measures what you've learned.

61
01:02:22,040 --> 01:02:23,960
But anyway, so that lasted for about 10 years.

62
01:02:23,960 --> 01:02:25,920
And then people started getting upset at the idea

63
01:02:25,920 --> 01:02:27,720
of different levels of achievements.

64
01:02:27,720 --> 01:02:32,320
And now, what does SAT stand for?

65
01:02:32,320 --> 01:02:33,640
Nothing.

66
01:02:33,640 --> 01:02:36,160
It stands for nothing, explicitly.

67
01:02:36,160 --> 01:02:38,040
SAT is just a name like Bob.

68
01:02:38,040 --> 01:02:39,760
It's capital letters.

69
01:02:39,760 --> 01:02:41,960
But it doesn't stand for anything.

70
01:02:41,960 --> 01:02:43,200
It stands for Jack.

71
01:02:43,200 --> 01:02:44,320
OK, so that.

72
01:02:44,320 --> 01:02:46,320
And no one has gotten mad at them

73
01:02:46,320 --> 01:02:49,760
for having the test that stands for nothing.

74
01:02:49,760 --> 01:02:52,520
That or the initials stand for nothing.

75
01:02:52,520 --> 01:02:53,440
All right.

76
01:02:53,440 --> 01:02:56,960
So anyway, so these tests have upset a whole lot of people.

77
01:02:56,960 --> 01:02:59,640
And many people got angry about them.

78
01:02:59,640 --> 01:03:03,520
So let's just go over the main objections.

79
01:03:03,520 --> 01:03:07,960
The first and biggest one is cultural bias.

80
01:03:07,960 --> 01:03:10,880
And this argument begins with some key facts, which

81
01:03:10,880 --> 01:03:13,480
are not in dispute among people who know the data, which

82
01:03:13,480 --> 01:03:15,560
is that there are large differences in performance

83
01:03:15,560 --> 01:03:17,000
and IQ tests.

84
01:03:17,000 --> 01:03:19,840
And Jews do about one standard deviation better than Gentiles,

85
01:03:19,840 --> 01:03:22,800
specifically Ashkenazi Jews, Jews from Eastern Europe.

86
01:03:22,800 --> 01:03:25,680
And blacks do about one standard deviation worse.

87
01:03:25,680 --> 01:03:28,520
Now, the critics say, sure, these are the facts.

88
01:03:28,520 --> 01:03:31,040
But the reason is cultural bias.

89
01:03:31,040 --> 01:03:33,520
Cultural bias.

90
01:03:33,520 --> 01:03:36,160
Cultural bias.

91
01:03:36,160 --> 01:03:38,960
And supposedly, the tests are measuring familiarity

92
01:03:38,960 --> 01:03:40,800
with middle class lifestyles rather than

93
01:03:40,800 --> 01:03:42,280
any actual intellectual ability.

94
01:03:42,280 --> 01:03:43,640
And when you make this case, it's

95
01:03:43,640 --> 01:03:47,520
common to go and reference some humorous questions from IQ

96
01:03:47,520 --> 01:03:49,200
tests from the 50s.

97
01:03:49,200 --> 01:03:51,400
So there's an old question of something like, yacht is

98
01:03:51,400 --> 01:03:55,480
to regatta, a ski chalet is to, all right,

99
01:03:55,480 --> 01:03:58,440
something is to ski chalet.

100
01:03:58,440 --> 01:04:01,040
All right, reading this, that's middle class lifestyle.

101
01:04:01,040 --> 01:04:03,600
Sounds more like the super rich.

102
01:04:03,600 --> 01:04:08,440
Like, how is it that like, tenement living Jews

103
01:04:08,440 --> 01:04:11,080
from New York City are getting exposure to all this stuff?

104
01:04:11,080 --> 01:04:11,960
I don't know exactly.

105
01:04:11,960 --> 01:04:14,400
But anyway, there were some questions

106
01:04:14,400 --> 01:04:18,040
that were pretty funny that do at least smell

107
01:04:18,040 --> 01:04:20,600
like very culturally linked questions.

108
01:04:20,600 --> 01:04:22,600
Although over time, people who write the tests

109
01:04:22,600 --> 01:04:25,560
have gotten very touchy about anything that might possibly

110
01:04:25,560 --> 01:04:27,240
be accused of cultural bias.

111
01:04:27,240 --> 01:04:30,440
So now, you're more likely to have squares and circles.

112
01:04:30,440 --> 01:04:32,600
That's not cultural, is it?

113
01:04:32,600 --> 01:04:33,840
Case.

114
01:04:33,840 --> 01:04:34,920
All right.

115
01:04:34,920 --> 01:04:38,880
So in any case, this cultural bias claim

116
01:04:38,880 --> 01:04:40,520
is empirically testable.

117
01:04:40,520 --> 01:04:42,600
And it has been tested, most prominently

118
01:04:42,600 --> 01:04:44,480
by the US military.

119
01:04:44,480 --> 01:04:47,520
So what would it mean to test for cultural bias?

120
01:04:47,520 --> 01:04:49,080
Well, general idea.

121
01:04:49,080 --> 01:04:52,760
Suppose that a test is unfairly biased against you.

122
01:04:52,760 --> 01:04:55,200
Then does your performance on that test

123
01:04:55,200 --> 01:04:59,000
understate or overstate your true ability?

124
01:04:59,000 --> 01:05:00,240
It understates it, yes.

125
01:05:00,240 --> 01:05:01,880
So a test is biased against you that

126
01:05:01,880 --> 01:05:03,440
understates your true ability.

127
01:05:03,440 --> 01:05:06,800
Like, suppose that there is an econ professor who hates you.

128
01:05:06,800 --> 01:05:08,480
When they give you an F, does this

129
01:05:08,480 --> 01:05:10,760
over or under predict your performance

130
01:05:10,760 --> 01:05:12,160
in other economics classes, given

131
01:05:12,160 --> 01:05:15,160
that the person was motivated by a true view?

132
01:05:15,160 --> 01:05:17,320
Under predicts you that you're expected to do better

133
01:05:17,320 --> 01:05:21,160
than the score that you got under these adverse circumstances

134
01:05:21,160 --> 01:05:22,720
would indicate.

135
01:05:22,720 --> 01:05:23,520
All right.

136
01:05:23,520 --> 01:05:27,280
So what the US military especially has done

137
01:05:27,280 --> 01:05:31,000
is they have a whole lot of experiments

138
01:05:31,000 --> 01:05:33,640
where you've got your paper and pencil IQ score here.

139
01:05:33,640 --> 01:05:35,760
And then you have your score on a performance test.

140
01:05:38,480 --> 01:05:40,400
And usually, these performance tests look nothing

141
01:05:40,400 --> 01:05:41,560
like paper and pencil tests.

142
01:05:41,560 --> 01:05:44,160
This would be like number of cones

143
01:05:44,160 --> 01:05:47,080
that you accidentally knock over when navigating a tank

144
01:05:47,080 --> 01:05:49,480
through an obstacle course after tank training,

145
01:05:49,480 --> 01:05:53,320
or some measure of flying a plane.

146
01:05:53,320 --> 01:05:55,520
Originally, there's nothing cultural about crashing

147
01:05:55,520 --> 01:05:56,020
a plane.

148
01:05:56,020 --> 01:05:58,240
Like, in my culture, the planes still airborne.

149
01:05:58,240 --> 01:05:59,080
No.

150
01:05:59,080 --> 01:06:00,760
There's nothing cultural about that.

151
01:06:00,760 --> 01:06:01,260
All right.

152
01:06:01,260 --> 01:06:03,960
So the big results from this kind of research.

153
01:06:03,960 --> 01:06:06,240
So first of all, as you might guess,

154
01:06:06,240 --> 01:06:09,280
IQ tests are predictive of performance on tests

155
01:06:09,280 --> 01:06:11,880
that don't seem like IQ.

156
01:06:11,880 --> 01:06:14,400
So your performance on an IQ test in particular

157
01:06:14,400 --> 01:06:17,120
is very predictive of trainability.

158
01:06:17,120 --> 01:06:18,520
It's one thing for a smart person

159
01:06:18,520 --> 01:06:21,320
to not know how to even open up a cockpit on a plane

160
01:06:21,320 --> 01:06:23,000
on the first day.

161
01:06:23,000 --> 01:06:24,520
That's totally possible.

162
01:06:24,520 --> 01:06:27,680
But after 12 weeks of training, how much

163
01:06:27,680 --> 01:06:29,400
has the person with a higher score

164
01:06:29,400 --> 01:06:33,040
learned versus the person with a lower score?

165
01:06:33,040 --> 01:06:35,320
So that is the key question that we're looking at.

166
01:06:35,320 --> 01:06:37,120
So we have especially a performance test

167
01:06:37,120 --> 01:06:39,080
at the end of some training period.

168
01:06:39,080 --> 01:06:41,080
And why does the military do so much of this?

169
01:06:41,080 --> 01:06:43,240
Because the training is really expensive.

170
01:06:43,240 --> 01:06:45,440
And they don't want to waste it on people

171
01:06:45,440 --> 01:06:47,760
that are on the train.

172
01:06:47,760 --> 01:06:49,400
Pretty simple, obvious point.

173
01:06:49,400 --> 01:06:51,940
Especially they don't want to go and put a $100 million plane

174
01:06:51,940 --> 01:06:54,280
in the hands of someone who's going to crash it.

175
01:06:54,280 --> 01:06:55,400
Right?

176
01:06:55,400 --> 01:06:58,440
So that's not too good.

177
01:06:58,440 --> 01:06:59,480
All right.

178
01:06:59,480 --> 01:07:01,560
So anyway, start off with a line like this.

179
01:07:01,560 --> 01:07:04,960
This is the typical line.

180
01:07:04,960 --> 01:07:07,520
Now, if the test is biased against your group,

181
01:07:07,520 --> 01:07:09,640
where should your line be?

182
01:07:09,640 --> 01:07:13,960
Remember, we said that you will do better than expected

183
01:07:13,960 --> 01:07:16,560
if the test is biased against you.

184
01:07:16,560 --> 01:07:18,520
So where should the line be if the test

185
01:07:18,520 --> 01:07:21,760
is biased against your group?

186
01:07:21,760 --> 01:07:24,280
That's what everyone wants to say.

187
01:07:24,280 --> 01:07:26,680
Because remember, the whole idea is for any given IQ,

188
01:07:26,680 --> 01:07:28,480
if the test is biased against you,

189
01:07:28,480 --> 01:07:30,280
or the IQ test is biased against you,

190
01:07:30,280 --> 01:07:32,800
you will be better at the practical task

191
01:07:32,800 --> 01:07:35,600
than the test indicates.

192
01:07:35,600 --> 01:07:42,240
And so this is what biased against looks like.

193
01:07:42,240 --> 01:07:45,440
So if the test is genuinely biased against your group,

194
01:07:45,440 --> 01:07:47,640
then your performance on the practical task

195
01:07:47,640 --> 01:07:50,000
should exceed what is expected given your performance

196
01:07:50,000 --> 01:07:51,760
on the paper and pencil test.

197
01:07:51,760 --> 01:07:53,840
And the big result from all this military research

198
01:07:53,840 --> 01:07:55,280
is that's not true.

199
01:07:55,280 --> 01:07:59,280
There's one progression line, at least for all groups

200
01:07:59,280 --> 01:08:01,160
that are native speakers of English.

201
01:08:01,160 --> 01:08:03,780
Now, notice, once you think about it this way,

202
01:08:03,780 --> 01:08:06,240
it's easy to see that there's nothing funny about cultural

203
01:08:06,240 --> 01:08:07,080
bias.

204
01:08:07,080 --> 01:08:10,160
There are plenty of tests that are culturally biased.

205
01:08:10,160 --> 01:08:13,840
Imagine I were to go and give you the final exam in Mandarin.

206
01:08:13,840 --> 01:08:15,320
So who here has flown in Mandarin?

207
01:08:15,320 --> 01:08:15,820
Who has?

208
01:08:15,820 --> 01:08:16,320
I don't.

209
01:08:16,320 --> 01:08:16,820
I don't.

210
01:08:16,820 --> 01:08:18,440
I don't either.

211
01:08:18,440 --> 01:08:20,080
All right, so anyway.

212
01:08:20,080 --> 01:08:22,600
So if I gave the final exam in Mandarin,

213
01:08:22,600 --> 01:08:25,200
and presumably would have to be graded by someone who also

214
01:08:25,200 --> 01:08:27,960
spoke Mandarin, but if I gave it in Mandarin,

215
01:08:27,960 --> 01:08:29,760
then everyone here who doesn't speak it

216
01:08:29,760 --> 01:08:32,200
would turn out to be as bad an economist as you can possibly

217
01:08:32,200 --> 01:08:34,520
be, right?

218
01:08:34,520 --> 01:08:36,960
Basically have a score of zero.

219
01:08:36,960 --> 01:08:41,600
And then everybody else would score vastly better than you.

220
01:08:41,600 --> 01:08:43,960
But if we were to go and use that performance on this one

221
01:08:43,960 --> 01:08:46,460
test to predict your performance in other economics classes,

222
01:08:46,460 --> 01:08:49,000
or your number of publications, or any other subsequent

223
01:08:49,000 --> 01:08:50,800
measure of performance, we would see

224
01:08:50,800 --> 01:08:53,760
that all the people that are not speakers of Mandarin

225
01:08:53,760 --> 01:08:56,480
would do way better than their terrible score indicates.

226
01:08:59,320 --> 01:09:02,000
So that's the idea.

227
01:09:02,000 --> 01:09:04,520
And if anything, there's a little bit of evidence

228
01:09:04,520 --> 01:09:08,120
the tests are biased against groups that do well on them,

229
01:09:08,120 --> 01:09:11,600
rather than against groups that do poorly on them.

230
01:09:11,600 --> 01:09:13,800
So this is a very mainstream result.

231
01:09:13,800 --> 01:09:15,600
This is one where the US military actually

232
01:09:15,600 --> 01:09:17,300
had press conferences where they came out

233
01:09:17,300 --> 01:09:19,600
and were like, who's the appropriate amount?

234
01:09:19,600 --> 01:09:20,600
And you're like, really?

235
01:09:20,600 --> 01:09:21,100
Really?

236
01:09:21,100 --> 01:09:22,080
Sorry.

237
01:09:22,080 --> 01:09:26,000
But this is what we found.

238
01:09:26,000 --> 01:09:29,120
All right, so imagine the awkwardness of that.

239
01:09:29,120 --> 01:09:32,960
So anyway, so cultural bias, it is a serious hypothesis.

240
01:09:32,960 --> 01:09:33,680
But it's tested.

241
01:09:33,680 --> 01:09:36,080
And because it's serious, it's testable.

242
01:09:36,080 --> 01:09:39,640
And the result is that it's wrong.

243
01:09:39,640 --> 01:09:41,040
Questions?

244
01:09:41,040 --> 01:09:45,960
Were these IQ tests, were they at the time of this?

245
01:09:45,960 --> 01:09:48,360
So generally, these would be your recruitment tests.

246
01:09:48,360 --> 01:09:51,640
But were these adjusted already, like after complaints

247
01:09:51,640 --> 01:09:53,600
of cultural bias?

248
01:09:53,600 --> 01:09:55,480
But see, yeah, yeah, yeah.

249
01:09:55,480 --> 01:09:57,680
So these would be tests generally

250
01:09:57,680 --> 01:09:59,240
from the 80s to 90s.

251
01:09:59,240 --> 01:10:02,120
So these are not, so I don't know that anyone did anything

252
01:10:02,120 --> 01:10:03,040
like this from the 50s.

253
01:10:05,680 --> 01:10:09,320
There has been some work, like how bad were the funny questions

254
01:10:09,320 --> 01:10:10,200
really?

255
01:10:10,200 --> 01:10:12,320
So I don't remember where the result is.

256
01:10:12,320 --> 01:10:13,240
I don't think I know.

257
01:10:13,240 --> 01:10:14,880
They're not as crazy as they seem.

258
01:10:14,880 --> 01:10:16,760
But again, especially the idea of how is it

259
01:10:16,760 --> 01:10:20,040
that Jewish kids growing up in tenements in New York

260
01:10:20,040 --> 01:10:22,960
know about regattas and stuff like that?

261
01:10:22,960 --> 01:10:23,720
Maybe from books.

262
01:10:23,720 --> 01:10:26,320
And again, but in particular, from reading books

263
01:10:26,320 --> 01:10:28,800
and then retaining the information.

264
01:10:28,800 --> 01:10:31,120
There's a general problem with retaining information

265
01:10:31,120 --> 01:10:34,000
or also even noticing that information's there.

266
01:10:34,000 --> 01:10:36,080
Ever notice the thing where you learn a new word

267
01:10:36,080 --> 01:10:38,160
and suddenly the new word is everywhere?

268
01:10:38,160 --> 01:10:40,320
Now, either the whole universe revolves around you,

269
01:10:40,320 --> 01:10:45,320
or what's the more reasonable theory about what was going on?

270
01:10:45,320 --> 01:10:49,400
The word was everywhere, both before and after.

271
01:10:49,400 --> 01:10:51,440
But in before and after, the word

272
01:10:51,440 --> 01:10:53,960
was before and after, but in before you

273
01:10:53,960 --> 01:10:58,560
knew about the word's existence, you didn't notice it.

274
01:10:58,560 --> 01:11:00,880
I don't notice things that I don't know about?

275
01:11:00,880 --> 01:11:02,400
Exactly.

276
01:11:02,400 --> 01:11:06,280
Things of which you know little, you are unaware of.

277
01:11:06,280 --> 01:11:09,520
Just like my older sons were completely

278
01:11:09,520 --> 01:11:14,360
unaware of any kind of dating going on in the K through 6

279
01:11:14,360 --> 01:11:18,400
scene, whereas my younger kids are hyper aware of it.

280
01:11:18,400 --> 01:11:20,320
And I'm like, well, it doesn't seem

281
01:11:20,320 --> 01:11:21,880
like there was nothing going on.

282
01:11:21,880 --> 01:11:26,040
I guess I was just so oblivious I didn't know it.

283
01:11:26,040 --> 01:11:27,800
If you weren't even thinking about it,

284
01:11:27,800 --> 01:11:29,920
then you just pass this through your awareness

285
01:11:29,920 --> 01:11:31,160
