01:22:00,360 --> 01:22:03,800
In terms of what actually correlates the best

48
01:22:03,800 --> 01:22:08,800
with the purest measure of intelligence,

49
01:22:08,920 --> 01:22:11,160
I mean, a lot of it is fairly counterintuitive

50
01:22:11,160 --> 01:22:12,000
to people initially.

51
01:22:12,000 --> 01:22:14,520
Like, for example, there's something called

52
01:22:14,520 --> 01:22:16,360
Raven's Progressive Matrices, which is intended

53
01:22:16,360 --> 01:22:19,520
to be one of the least cultural tests ever designed.

54
01:22:19,520 --> 01:22:22,360
And this is one of those highly predictive things,

55
01:22:22,360 --> 01:22:24,000
but something that's almost as good

56
01:22:24,000 --> 01:22:25,380
as just a vocabulary test.

57
01:22:26,600 --> 01:22:29,480
All right, like, vocabulary, but that's ultra-cultural.

58
01:22:29,480 --> 01:22:32,080
It's like, well, the idea is that if you grow up

59
01:22:32,080 --> 01:22:34,440
within a given language group,

60
01:22:34,440 --> 01:22:36,080
people with high intelligence are likely

61
01:22:36,080 --> 01:22:37,560
to not only learn more words,

62
01:22:37,560 --> 01:22:40,060
but they're likely to learn more conceptually complex words,

63
01:22:40,060 --> 01:22:41,880
and not only to know the sounds,

64
01:22:41,880 --> 01:22:43,440
but to actually be able to apply them.

65
01:22:43,440 --> 01:22:44,640
So we can actually, I think specifically,

66
01:22:44,640 --> 01:22:47,840
it's like the analogies, like the vocabulary-based analogies.

67
01:22:47,840 --> 01:22:49,120
You ever taken, I don't know, like,

68
01:22:49,120 --> 01:22:52,480
is that still on the SAT, like analogies?

69
01:22:52,480 --> 01:22:55,040
So, yeah, that's one where it seems to be,

70
01:22:55,040 --> 01:22:57,320
like, what researchers call highly G-loaded.

71
01:22:57,320 --> 01:23:00,520
So again, what I like, what I would say is that

72
01:23:00,520 --> 01:23:01,800
in the real world, there's no such thing

73
01:23:01,800 --> 01:23:04,060
as a test of pure intelligence.

74
01:23:04,060 --> 01:23:06,320
Like, I'm certainly about to, however,

75
01:23:06,320 --> 01:23:08,520
like, we don't need to be that good, actually.

76
01:23:08,520 --> 01:23:09,740
In the real world, you just have to settle

77
01:23:09,740 --> 01:23:11,000
for something quite a bit worse than that,

78
01:23:11,000 --> 01:23:13,880
but the quite a bit worse thing is still pretty good.

79
01:23:13,880 --> 01:23:16,600
So, you know, like, I mean, in general,

80
01:23:16,600 --> 01:23:18,600
like, if you were to find someone that,

81
01:23:18,600 --> 01:23:20,840
you know, is in the same language culture as you,

82
01:23:20,840 --> 01:23:23,760
and they are unaware of how many states there are,

83
01:23:23,760 --> 01:23:24,960
you might say, well, that's something you learn in school.

84
01:23:24,960 --> 01:23:25,840
It doesn't show that you're smart.

85
01:23:25,840 --> 01:23:27,880
Like, well, it does if you're told 50 times,

86
01:23:27,880 --> 01:23:29,600
but you still don't know it.

87
01:23:29,600 --> 01:23:31,200
Right, so that's the kind of thing.

88
01:23:31,200 --> 01:23:32,280
You get an interview, I'm like,

89
01:23:32,280 --> 01:23:34,400
if you were to go and, like, make me go

90
01:23:34,400 --> 01:23:36,360
and define intelligence, I had this one interviewer,

91
01:23:36,360 --> 01:23:39,560
I don't know, who just wanted to talk about definitions,

92
01:23:39,560 --> 01:23:41,920
and I'm like, hmm, all right, right, define this.

93
01:23:41,920 --> 01:23:43,120
I'm like, all right, well,

94
01:23:43,120 --> 01:23:44,720
he's using it the same way other people are using it,

95
01:23:44,720 --> 01:23:46,280
but I had to define intelligence,

96
01:23:46,280 --> 01:23:48,120
I've got learning ability.

97
01:23:48,120 --> 01:23:51,200
Learning ability, so propensity to learn.

98
01:23:51,200 --> 01:23:52,680
If you have something new comes along,

99
01:23:52,680 --> 01:23:56,040
how quickly and thoroughly and deeply do you learn it?

100
01:23:56,040 --> 01:23:57,480
So, and if you think of it that way,

101
01:23:57,480 --> 01:23:59,480
then measuring learning ability

102
01:23:59,480 --> 01:24:02,440
based upon amount that you know is not crazy.

103
01:24:02,440 --> 01:24:03,600
There is a logic to it.

104
01:24:04,600 --> 01:24:06,080
And yet, of course, if someone grew up

105
01:24:06,080 --> 01:24:07,120
in a totally different culture,

106
01:24:07,120 --> 01:24:08,200
then they were learning something else,

107
01:24:08,200 --> 01:24:10,120
and then, yeah, then there actually could be

108
01:24:10,120 --> 01:24:11,560
that cultural bias issue.

109
01:24:11,560 --> 01:24:13,960
The main result out of this is that

110
01:24:13,960 --> 01:24:16,280
you've gotta have, the culture's gotta be very different

111
01:24:16,280 --> 01:24:18,400
before the cultural bias is relevant.

112
01:24:18,400 --> 01:24:19,360
Right to the level of,

113
01:24:19,360 --> 01:24:21,640
you're not a native speaker of the language.

114
01:24:21,640 --> 01:24:23,480
And then, then the cultural bias

115
01:24:23,480 --> 01:24:25,720
turns out to be quite relevant.

116
01:24:25,720 --> 01:24:28,200
So, go and take a Spanish language pilot

117
01:24:28,200 --> 01:24:31,040
and have him, go and give him a test of piloting in English,

118
01:24:31,040 --> 01:24:33,400
and he's gonna do poorly, but he doesn't crash the plane.

119
01:24:33,400 --> 01:24:34,720
Showing that the English language test

120
01:24:34,720 --> 01:24:35,960
was culturally biased against him,

121
01:24:35,960 --> 01:24:37,400
that's a non-native speaker.

122
01:24:39,800 --> 01:24:42,400
All right, so, yes.

123
01:24:42,400 --> 01:24:47,400
So, each of the IQ test was created by some guy?

124
01:24:49,400 --> 01:24:50,240
Indeed, yes.

125
01:24:50,240 --> 01:24:53,760
A core panel of people, but yeah.

126
01:24:53,760 --> 01:24:57,520
So, if we let this guy who invented this test

127
01:24:57,520 --> 01:25:00,760
to take the test, will he get a very high,

128
01:25:00,760 --> 01:25:01,720
like, full score?

129
01:25:01,720 --> 01:25:04,480
Ah, the answer is yes, because normally, again,

130
01:25:04,480 --> 01:25:07,400
these tests are written by a committee, not one individual.

131
01:25:07,400 --> 01:25:10,760
And, again, for a test where thousands of people

132
01:25:10,760 --> 01:25:13,600
are taking it, and where people get sued,

133
01:25:13,600 --> 01:25:16,040
there's a lot of care taken into the test.

134
01:25:16,040 --> 01:25:17,520
There's a lot of care, so.

135
01:25:17,520 --> 01:25:19,000
Meaning, like, you can see this,

136
01:25:19,000 --> 01:25:21,280
if you ever get an old SAT test,

137
01:25:21,280 --> 01:25:23,600
versus you buy a commercial prep book,

138
01:25:23,600 --> 01:25:24,720
where someone made up, you know,

139
01:25:24,720 --> 01:25:26,840
like, made up, you know, some more questions,

140
01:25:26,840 --> 01:25:30,140
you'll find a lot of mistakes in those prep books, usually.

141
01:25:30,140 --> 01:25:32,280
The prep books are written by one or two people,

142
01:25:32,280 --> 01:25:34,880
and they knew no one was going to sue them

143
01:25:34,880 --> 01:25:36,400
if there was a mistake.

144
01:25:36,400 --> 01:25:39,720
Or, more often, there's just very debatable questions.

145
01:25:39,720 --> 01:25:41,560
But, for the actual one, there's probably

146
01:25:41,560 --> 01:25:43,840
a committee of 20 people going over it,

147
01:25:43,840 --> 01:25:46,400
and there's one or two people who say it's debatable,

148
01:25:46,400 --> 01:25:50,040
and they change the question, so that you remove all debate.

149
01:25:50,040 --> 01:25:52,760
So, like, when I'm doing standardized test practice

150
01:25:52,760 --> 01:25:56,520
with my kids, and they do a test prep book,

151
01:25:56,520 --> 01:25:57,760
every now and then, there'll be a question

152
01:25:57,760 --> 01:26:00,360
where they're saying, this answer's bogus.

153
01:26:00,360 --> 01:26:02,420
And I'll go, and I'll read it, and go, you're right.

154
01:26:02,420 --> 01:26:04,600
I rule the answer bogus, right?

155
01:26:04,600 --> 01:26:07,280
There's a ruling of bogosity on this.

156
01:26:07,280 --> 01:26:12,280
But, when there's an actual, real test from a previous year,

157
01:26:12,680 --> 01:26:13,960
I don't think I've ever done this,

158
01:26:13,960 --> 01:26:15,720
because, you know, like, this is one you read,

159
01:26:15,720 --> 01:26:17,200
and you say, hmm, seems a little bogus,

160
01:26:17,200 --> 01:26:19,520
and you go, ah, no.

161
01:26:19,520 --> 01:26:21,440
20 people vetted this.

162
01:26:21,440 --> 01:26:25,480
They look at that word that they put in there

163
01:26:25,480 --> 01:26:28,920
to make sure you couldn't read the question your way.

164
01:26:28,920 --> 01:26:30,720
Ha, very impressive.

165
01:26:33,840 --> 01:26:35,120
All right, so, in any case,

166
01:26:35,120 --> 01:26:36,320
Otto, do you have a question?

167
01:26:36,320 --> 01:26:37,800
What, no, I'm sorry, okay.

168
01:26:37,800 --> 01:26:39,680
All right, so, if you wanted to go,

169
01:26:39,680 --> 01:26:42,720
try analogies, intelligence is a lot like strength.

170
01:26:42,720 --> 01:26:44,160
Or, if you play Dungeons and Dragons,

171
01:26:44,160 --> 01:26:45,480
it's a lot like, also like,

172
01:26:45,480 --> 01:26:47,560
wisdom, dexterity, constitution, and charisma.

173
01:26:47,560 --> 01:26:50,880
All right, so, there's some ambiguity.

174
01:26:50,880 --> 01:26:52,280
Like, who's stronger?

175
01:26:52,280 --> 01:26:54,280
Like, a really great football player,

176
01:26:54,280 --> 01:26:56,840
or a really great basketball player?

177
01:26:56,840 --> 01:26:58,000
And there, you might say, well,

178
01:26:58,000 --> 01:26:59,680
each has some different kinds of strength.

179
01:26:59,680 --> 01:27:02,420
So, the football player has more crushing strength,

180
01:27:02,420 --> 01:27:04,940
but the basketball player has more jumping strength.

181
01:27:04,940 --> 01:27:06,880
All right, so, there's some ambiguity.

182
01:27:06,880 --> 01:27:09,600
But, we basically know what we mean,

183
01:27:09,600 --> 01:27:11,920
and we know there's real differences.

184
01:27:11,920 --> 01:27:12,760
If someone were to say,

185
01:27:12,760 --> 01:27:14,520
I'm stronger than any professional athlete,

186
01:27:14,520 --> 01:27:16,680
that would just be stupid, right?

187
01:27:16,680 --> 01:27:17,520
Like, I'm just not,

188
01:27:17,520 --> 01:27:21,040
and there's no arguing way around that, right?

189
01:27:21,040 --> 01:27:22,640
And people who are strong by one measure

190
01:27:22,640 --> 01:27:24,320
are usually strong by other measures, too,

191
01:27:24,320 --> 01:27:28,160
even if there isn't a perfect correlation in strength.

192
01:27:28,160 --> 01:27:29,780
And if someone were to go around saying,

193
01:27:29,780 --> 01:27:30,760
I don't know what strength means,

194
01:27:30,760 --> 01:27:34,000
or strength is arbitrary, or it's just a cultural thing,

195
01:27:34,000 --> 01:27:37,240
this is where you would probably be inclined to say,

196
01:27:37,240 --> 01:27:39,720
you sound like you really have an ax to grind here.

197
01:27:39,720 --> 01:27:41,720
You're very upset by the idea

198
01:27:41,720 --> 01:27:44,320
that some people are stronger than other people.

199
01:27:44,320 --> 01:27:46,200
And you would probably tend to assume

200
01:27:46,200 --> 01:27:48,400
the person felt insecure and had some issue,

201
01:27:48,400 --> 01:27:50,200
or was very touchy about something.

202
01:27:51,800 --> 01:27:52,720
All right, now, the second debate

203
01:27:52,720 --> 01:27:55,960
about the extent to which IQ is registered environmental,

204
01:27:55,960 --> 01:27:59,040
totally irrelevant for almost all of economics.

205
01:27:59,040 --> 01:28:00,920
But I do have a book on the subject,

206
01:28:00,920 --> 01:28:04,240
so I will give you a brief intro here.

207
01:28:04,240 --> 01:28:05,360
There is a lot of evidence

208
01:28:05,360 --> 01:28:08,680
from carefully constructed twin adoption studies

209
01:28:08,680 --> 01:28:11,480
that the variance is about 80% genetic,

210
01:28:11,480 --> 01:28:13,440
and the remaining 20% is very hard

211
01:28:13,440 --> 01:28:15,600
to figure out where it's coming from.

212
01:28:15,600 --> 01:28:18,000
So there's very little that seems to be based upon

213
01:28:18,000 --> 01:28:19,040
what family you grew up in,

214
01:28:19,040 --> 01:28:22,180
which we can see from looking at kids that are adopted.

215
01:28:23,240 --> 01:28:24,500
All of this research, however,

216
01:28:24,500 --> 01:28:26,780
is from within the first world.

217
01:28:26,780 --> 01:28:29,080
So if you, it's basically situations

218
01:28:29,080 --> 01:28:31,320
where you're growing up in one first world family

219
01:28:31,320 --> 01:28:33,220
rather than another first world family,

220
01:28:33,220 --> 01:28:36,360
and that doesn't seem to matter very much for intelligence.

221
01:28:36,360 --> 01:28:39,320
On the other hand, if you're talking about being raised,

222
01:28:39,320 --> 01:28:43,640
you're growing up in an orphanage in India or in Sweden,

223
01:28:43,640 --> 01:28:45,640
then that, it seems plausible that it would make

224
01:28:45,640 --> 01:28:48,200
a big difference, and I have some blog posts

225
01:28:48,200 --> 01:28:49,440
where I actually snap together

226
01:28:49,440 --> 01:28:51,220
a couple different bodies of literature

227
01:28:51,220 --> 01:28:53,240
to show that there does seem to be a very large effect

228
01:28:53,240 --> 01:28:56,200
of not growing up in an orphanage in India,

229
01:28:56,200 --> 01:28:57,800
and rather growing up in Sweden.

230
01:28:57,800 --> 01:29:00,320
So it's very interesting literature,

231
01:29:00,320 --> 01:29:01,840
because basically there's a lot of literature

232
01:29:01,840 --> 01:29:05,520
on how international adoptees in Scandinavia do.

233
01:29:05,520 --> 01:29:06,400
Why Scandinavia?

234
01:29:06,400 --> 01:29:09,360
Because they are the best record-keeping countries

235
01:29:09,360 --> 01:29:10,180
on the planet.

236
01:29:11,440 --> 01:29:13,320
So they've also got records on when exactly

237
01:29:13,320 --> 01:29:14,680
we're adopted and so on.

238
01:29:14,680 --> 01:29:16,400
Now most of this research just sits around

239
01:29:16,400 --> 01:29:19,380
comparing the international adoptees to native-born Swedes,

240
01:29:19,380 --> 01:29:21,480
and then comes around saying they're worse.

241
01:29:22,400 --> 01:29:25,220
What I did is I actually went and found out

242
01:29:25,220 --> 01:29:26,640
what would you expect the scores

243
01:29:26,640 --> 01:29:27,960
of those international adoptees to be

244
01:29:27,960 --> 01:29:31,040
if they were just average people from their home countries,

245
01:29:31,040 --> 01:29:34,440
and if you assume that, then international adoption

246
01:29:34,440 --> 01:29:37,180
at birth wipes out about 40% of a very large gap.

247
01:29:38,920 --> 01:29:40,720
The true effect is actually probably a lot larger,

248
01:29:40,720 --> 01:29:44,860
because do people generally adopt an average kid from India?

249
01:29:45,760 --> 01:29:47,120
You know, you adopt kids that probably

250
01:29:47,120 --> 01:29:48,400
would have been growing up in orphanages

251
01:29:48,400 --> 01:29:50,220
and other horribly disadvantaged circumstances,

252
01:29:50,220 --> 01:29:52,400
so this says that moving from India,

253
01:29:52,400 --> 01:29:54,700
the environmental effect of moving from India to Sweden

254
01:29:54,700 --> 01:29:58,880
is probably enormous, but I do look around,

255
01:29:58,880 --> 01:30:01,200
and no one ever bothered to snap together

256
01:30:01,200 --> 01:30:03,720
the two literatures, because they're just talking about,

257
01:30:03,720 --> 01:30:05,520
or not talking to each other.

258
01:30:05,520 --> 01:30:06,520
You have a question?

259
01:30:06,520 --> 01:30:07,360
Yeah.

260
01:30:07,360 --> 01:30:08,200
Yeah, please.

261
01:30:08,200 --> 01:30:11,600
In first-world studies, is there a big spread

262
01:30:11,600 --> 01:30:14,480
in the types of families that adopt?

263
01:30:14,480 --> 01:30:16,880
Like, do you compare twins that go to,

264
01:30:16,880 --> 01:30:19,000
like, you know, the families in the bottom 10% versus

265
01:30:19,000 --> 01:30:19,840
the ones that go to the top 10%?

266
01:30:19,840 --> 01:30:20,660
So that's a good question.

267
01:30:20,660 --> 01:30:22,760
So the basic thing is there's actually

268
01:30:22,760 --> 01:30:24,000
two totally separate literatures.

269
01:30:24,000 --> 01:30:25,880
There's adoption and there's twins.

270
01:30:25,880 --> 01:30:28,720
And then there's a subset of twins put up for adoption,

271
01:30:28,720 --> 01:30:31,280
but that's a very small number of people.

272
01:30:31,280 --> 01:30:34,280
So actually, there's, like, the main methods are just,

273
01:30:34,280 --> 01:30:37,840
the main method is just to take a singleton, one kid,

274
01:30:37,840 --> 01:30:39,360
he's born in one biological family,

275
01:30:39,360 --> 01:30:40,500
send him off to another family,

276
01:30:40,500 --> 01:30:42,200
and then they adopt him and they raise him,

277
01:30:42,200 --> 01:30:44,080
and then you go and look at the correlation

278
01:30:44,080 --> 01:30:46,760
between the traits of the adopting family and the adoptee.

279
01:30:46,760 --> 01:30:48,280
So, you know, like that, and that has nothing to do

280
01:30:48,280 --> 01:30:50,400
with twins, and then the twin studies are usually

281
01:30:50,400 --> 01:30:53,060
about twins that are raised by their biological parents,

282
01:30:53,060 --> 01:30:55,280
but some are identical and some are fraternal.

283
01:30:55,280 --> 01:30:57,200
And then, yes, and then that's where you get

284
01:30:57,200 --> 01:30:59,520
the identification, then there's this much smaller number

285
01:30:59,520 --> 01:31:03,400
of highly publicized twin-separated birth studies,

286
01:31:03,400 --> 01:31:06,160
which are really cool, but, and they're fun to read about,

287
01:31:06,160 --> 01:31:08,600
but it's only a very tiny bit of data.

288
01:31:09,520 --> 01:31:14,520
But your deeper question really is how much range

289
01:31:14,520 --> 01:31:17,200
is there in the kind of families you get adopted by?

290
01:31:17,200 --> 01:31:20,960
And the answer there is that in recent decades,

291
01:31:20,960 --> 01:31:22,800
like, almost everyone has at least been a class,

292
01:31:22,800 --> 01:31:25,440
so you are cutting out a lot of distribution,

293
01:31:25,440 --> 01:31:29,160
but in the 50s and 60s, there was a wider range,

294
01:31:29,160 --> 01:31:31,760
and the adoption studies that were done on those families

295
01:31:31,760 --> 01:31:33,640
seem to get pretty similar results.

296
01:31:33,640 --> 01:31:35,640
So basically, there's one very famous study

297
01:31:35,640 --> 01:31:38,520
of Korean orphans that got adopted,

298
01:31:38,520 --> 01:31:40,720
and for the Americans to be eligible,

299
01:31:40,720 --> 01:31:43,480
they only had to be 25% above the poverty line.

300
01:31:43,480 --> 01:31:46,840
So you're really talking about a very wide range of families,

301
01:31:46,840 --> 01:31:49,840
although, of course, what you don't see in adoption studies

302
01:31:49,840 --> 01:31:53,560
are families that hate kids, right?

303
01:31:53,560 --> 01:31:55,160
So you don't see any families like that.

304
01:31:55,160 --> 01:31:57,720
Well, these families that knew they would hate kids,

