363
01:74:29,000 --> 01:74:33,160
able to do the job, but there's no tendency at all to

364
01:74:33,160 --> 01:74:35,960
go and reduce the wage of existing workers and then

365
01:74:35,960 --> 01:74:37,960
hire this new surplus labor.

366
01:74:37,960 --> 01:74:40,120
Instead, it seems like you've got the insiders who keep

367
01:74:40,120 --> 01:74:41,400
their nice jobs.

368
01:74:41,400 --> 01:74:44,720
And then, in fact, their jobs got better.

369
01:74:44,720 --> 01:74:47,240
So if you worked at a government-sponsored

370
01:74:47,240 --> 01:74:50,240
enterprise during this time, your job improved a lot.

371
01:74:50,240 --> 01:74:52,960
Because your pay was essentially the same, and your

372
01:74:52,960 --> 01:74:55,320
workload fell to almost nothing.

373
01:74:55,320 --> 01:74:58,920
So it's like an ideal job, really.

374
01:74:58,920 --> 01:75:02,040
Great job security, great pay, no work.

375
01:75:02,040 --> 01:75:03,280
Wonderful.

376
01:75:05,240 --> 01:75:07,920
So what's going on?

377
01:75:07,920 --> 01:75:10,400
So there is a neoclassical way that you

378
01:75:10,400 --> 01:75:11,280
could explain all this.

379
01:75:11,280 --> 01:75:15,360
And that's to say the problem is negative costs.

380
01:75:15,360 --> 01:75:17,840
So there's negative costs.

381
01:75:17,840 --> 01:75:20,160
You say, why don't we adjust the wage?

382
01:75:20,160 --> 01:75:23,240
Because the cost of adjusting the wage is too high.

383
01:75:23,240 --> 01:75:26,880
It's like, why don't you go and adjust the price of steak

384
01:75:26,880 --> 01:75:28,880
so that you never sell out your steak?

385
01:75:28,880 --> 01:75:31,280
Well, because the cost of going and printing up a new

386
01:75:31,280 --> 01:75:33,520
menu at the restaurant every single day or

387
01:75:33,520 --> 01:75:35,080
every hour is so high.

388
01:75:35,080 --> 01:75:36,760
So instead, we just have to sell out on it

389
01:75:36,760 --> 01:75:38,120
based on high demand.

390
01:75:38,120 --> 01:75:39,120
Make sense?

391
01:75:39,120 --> 01:75:41,680
Ever wonder why a restaurant sells out on something?

392
01:75:41,680 --> 01:75:43,600
Why didn't they just raise the price to make sure that it

393
01:75:43,600 --> 01:75:46,200
would be available for you?

394
01:75:46,200 --> 01:75:47,960
Menu costs are a pretty obvious story.

395
01:75:47,960 --> 01:75:50,080
It would be such a pain in the neck to go and change the

396
01:75:50,080 --> 01:75:53,160
menus around that they'd rather just occasionally run

397
01:75:53,160 --> 01:75:55,400
out.

398
01:75:55,400 --> 01:75:57,840
And you tell the same story about labor markets.

399
01:75:57,840 --> 01:76:01,600
The reason why employers don't go and cut the wages during

400
01:76:01,600 --> 01:76:05,320
recession is because the menu costs of going and updating

401
01:76:05,320 --> 01:76:07,840
the website or whatever would be too high.

402
01:76:07,840 --> 01:76:11,160
And so you just tolerate the situation of the surplus.

403
01:76:16,240 --> 01:76:19,480
But this menu cost story seems pretty hard to believe for

404
01:76:19,480 --> 01:76:23,320
labor markets because the menu costs seem very small

405
01:76:23,320 --> 01:76:24,840
relative to the value of the product.

406
01:76:24,840 --> 01:76:26,400
It's one thing to say we're not going to print up all new

407
01:76:26,400 --> 01:76:30,440
menus rather than waste $30 worth of steak.

408
01:76:30,440 --> 01:76:34,000
But to say we're not going to go and adjust our firm web

409
01:76:34,000 --> 01:76:37,920
page just because there's a million dollars of perfectly

410
01:76:37,920 --> 01:76:40,880
good labor sitting around on the market, how much does your

411
01:76:40,880 --> 01:76:43,200
web page cost to update?

412
01:76:43,200 --> 01:76:44,400
What's the big deal here?

413
01:76:44,400 --> 01:76:46,400
How hard can it possibly be?

414
01:76:46,400 --> 01:76:48,520
It doesn't seem like the menu costs are all that

415
01:76:48,520 --> 01:76:49,720
substantial.

416
01:76:49,720 --> 01:76:51,160
So that's the puzzle.

417
01:76:55,080 --> 01:76:58,640
And furthermore, by the way, the menu cost model predicts

418
01:76:58,640 --> 01:77:05,680
that while you may not adjust your prices to respond to

419
01:77:05,680 --> 01:77:09,560
small market changes in supplier demand, you will for

420
01:77:09,560 --> 01:77:12,640
large changes.

421
01:77:12,640 --> 01:77:15,640
The idea is that for a small change, then you'll say, well,

422
01:77:15,640 --> 01:77:19,040
the amount of surplus or shortage is so small, we're

423
01:77:19,040 --> 01:77:22,480
only losing a little bit of money, and that money is

424
01:77:22,480 --> 01:77:24,840
smaller than the menu costs.

425
01:77:24,840 --> 01:77:28,280
But if there were a demand increase by a factor of 10, in

426
01:77:28,280 --> 01:77:30,240
that case, you're going to go and change it.

427
01:77:30,240 --> 01:77:33,120
Because if you didn't, you'd be losing a lot of revenue,

428
01:77:33,120 --> 01:77:34,380
losing a lot of money.

429
01:77:37,640 --> 01:77:41,320
What this means, then, is that really big shocks to labor

430
01:77:41,320 --> 01:77:47,400
demand should lead to the end of labor market rigidity.

431
01:77:47,400 --> 01:77:52,280
So how does that work out for the Great Recession?

432
01:77:52,280 --> 01:77:55,320
It's the biggest shock since the Great Depression.

433
01:77:55,320 --> 01:77:56,720
And was it over?

434
01:77:56,720 --> 01:77:59,680
Did we get out of it really quickly?

435
01:77:59,680 --> 01:78:00,440
No.

436
01:78:00,440 --> 01:78:03,280
It seemed like it actually fit the standard long run pattern

437
01:78:03,280 --> 01:78:05,400
of about a percentage point fall per year.

438
01:78:05,400 --> 01:78:06,320
How about the Great Depression?

439
01:78:06,320 --> 01:78:07,840
Was that quick?

440
01:78:07,840 --> 01:78:08,720
No?

441
01:78:08,720 --> 01:78:09,160
All right, yeah.

442
01:78:09,160 --> 01:78:10,800
So the Great Depression, it wasn't just that unemployment

443
01:78:10,800 --> 01:78:12,440
went up to 25%.

444
01:78:12,440 --> 01:78:15,280
It stayed really high for a long time.

445
01:78:15,280 --> 01:78:17,240
Whereas the menu cost story should say that when there's

446
01:78:17,240 --> 01:78:20,080
that big of a change, we'll then prevent the menus.

447
01:78:20,080 --> 01:78:22,080
We'll just print out new menus.

448
01:78:22,080 --> 01:78:24,960
And that seems like it didn't happen.

449
01:78:24,960 --> 01:78:30,520
Which brings us to a more popular story, which

450
01:78:30,520 --> 01:78:32,040
were actually two different stories.

451
01:78:32,040 --> 01:78:34,680
So two behavioral stories about what's going on,

452
01:78:34,680 --> 01:78:39,840
one of which says that there is some kind of money illusion

453
01:78:39,840 --> 01:78:43,360
that workers experience.

454
01:78:43,360 --> 01:78:45,560
And the other one says there's some kind of issue

455
01:78:45,560 --> 01:78:46,840
with wage fairness norms.

456
01:78:46,840 --> 01:78:47,320
All right.

457
01:78:52,760 --> 01:78:53,560
OK.

458
01:78:53,560 --> 01:78:54,840
All right.

459
01:78:54,840 --> 01:78:56,600
So what's the evidence for this?

460
01:78:56,600 --> 01:78:58,400
Well, there's a bunch of psychological studies

461
01:78:58,400 --> 01:79:00,120
that find that people do seem to have money

462
01:79:00,120 --> 01:79:01,120
illusion to some degree.

463
01:79:04,400 --> 01:79:07,320
Essentially, for modest inflation rates,

464
01:79:07,320 --> 01:79:10,760
people just seem to round it down to zero.

465
01:79:10,760 --> 01:79:14,320
So that's pretty straightforward.

466
01:79:14,320 --> 01:79:18,080
But what's striking is even when you make it totally explicit,

467
01:79:18,080 --> 01:79:20,240
like you just spell it out for them so that you get rid

468
01:79:20,240 --> 01:79:22,040
of money illusion, it still seems

469
01:79:22,040 --> 01:79:27,480
like people see a difference between a nominal pay cut

470
01:79:27,480 --> 01:79:34,560
and a real pay cut in the presence of high inflation.

471
01:79:34,560 --> 01:79:37,880
So here is a well-known experiment.

472
01:79:37,880 --> 01:79:39,560
So they just ask people to evaluate

473
01:79:39,560 --> 01:79:41,840
the behavior of two firms.

474
01:79:41,840 --> 01:79:46,240
And they specify they're both making small profits.

475
01:79:46,240 --> 01:79:48,440
So the first hypothetical is inflation is zero,

476
01:79:48,440 --> 01:79:51,800
and everyone gets a 7% pay cut.

477
01:79:51,800 --> 01:79:55,200
Fraction of Americans who say it's unfair, 62%.

478
01:79:55,200 --> 01:79:56,240
Terrible.

479
01:79:56,240 --> 01:79:59,880
Next hypothetical, inflation is 12%, and you get a 5% raise.

480
01:79:59,880 --> 01:80:12,440
Boy, that's a lot of money.


1
01:80:00,000 --> 01:80:02,560
Only 22% say that's unfair.

2
01:80:02,560 --> 01:80:04,280
So this is one where you're really spelling it out

3
01:80:04,280 --> 01:80:05,520
for people.

4
01:80:05,520 --> 01:80:08,560
You're saying, here's the raise, here's the inflation.

5
01:80:08,560 --> 01:80:11,520
And yet, people regard these as being very different.

6
01:80:11,520 --> 01:80:13,600
Seems like there's something, for most people,

7
01:80:13,600 --> 01:80:18,600
there's a moral barrier at the actual cut in your base pay.

8
01:80:19,840 --> 01:80:22,480
And again, so there is some wiggle room in a few ways,

9
01:80:22,480 --> 01:80:26,040
like most people regard it as less unfair to slash a bonus.

10
01:80:26,040 --> 01:80:29,060
But the thing is, the base hourly pay rate,

11
01:80:29,060 --> 01:80:31,600
that's the thing where people really freak out.

12
01:80:32,440 --> 01:80:35,520
So it's also considered not that unfair

13
01:80:35,520 --> 01:80:37,440
to cut everybody's hours

14
01:80:37,440 --> 01:80:39,600
while giving them the same wage per hour.

15
01:80:39,600 --> 01:80:40,420
That's okay.

16
01:80:41,720 --> 01:80:44,280
But not okay to have them work the same number of hours

17
01:80:44,280 --> 01:80:48,280
for a smaller pay, which is satanic.

18
01:80:48,280 --> 01:80:49,360
It's just very evil.

19
01:80:50,960 --> 01:80:53,560
Now, a very reasonable or honest reaction is to say,

20
01:80:53,560 --> 01:80:55,120
well, who cares about fairness?

21
01:80:55,120 --> 01:80:56,400
We're not in the fairness business,

22
01:80:56,400 --> 01:80:58,080
we're in the making money business.

23
01:80:58,080 --> 01:81:00,040
What difference does it make?

24
01:81:00,040 --> 01:81:00,960
But there, the answer is,

25
01:81:00,960 --> 01:81:04,040
there's also evidence, surprisingly,

26
01:81:04,040 --> 01:81:07,800
that worker disgruntled hurts productivity.

27
01:81:08,880 --> 01:81:11,440
When workers are unhappy, they are less productive.

28
01:81:12,520 --> 01:81:15,240
All right, so if you saw the seminar,

29
01:81:15,240 --> 01:81:20,240
I think it was actually fall of 2016.

30
01:81:20,480 --> 01:81:22,360
So this is Truman Buley, who has a book called

31
01:81:22,360 --> 01:81:24,740
Why Wages Don't Fall During Recession.

32
01:81:24,740 --> 01:81:28,320
So it was not the best seminar I've ever seen.

33
01:81:28,320 --> 01:81:30,520
He wouldn't even speak up to an audible volume.

34
01:81:30,520 --> 01:81:33,360
So if you were there and felt cheated,

35
01:81:33,360 --> 01:81:34,680
we'll all say, look, the book's better.

36
01:81:34,680 --> 01:81:36,760
It's a lot, the book is really good.

37
01:81:36,760 --> 01:81:38,920
It's well-written, it's insightful.

38
01:81:38,920 --> 01:81:40,720
I was not happy with that seminar.

39
01:81:44,080 --> 01:81:46,000
I was in the front row, I couldn't hear.

40
01:81:46,000 --> 01:81:47,840
And no matter how many times I asked him to speak louder,

41
01:81:47,840 --> 01:81:49,000
he just wouldn't do it.

42
01:81:50,320 --> 01:81:51,160
Maybe you should have told me

43
01:81:51,160 --> 01:81:54,480
that you had a sub-audible volume.

44
01:81:54,480 --> 01:81:56,440
Or at least, if you told me that,

45
01:81:56,440 --> 01:81:57,680
I guess I could have gotten a microphone.

46
01:81:57,680 --> 01:81:59,440
Anyway, that was the,

47
01:81:59,440 --> 01:82:01,480
it was a long run, I really was embarrassed in the end.

48
01:82:01,480 --> 01:82:02,920
But the book, I stand by the book.

49
01:82:02,920 --> 01:82:04,340
The book's great.

50
01:82:04,340 --> 01:82:06,240
And he spent a lot of time talking to people

51
01:82:06,240 --> 01:82:08,980
actually who are involved in employment.

52
01:82:08,980 --> 01:82:11,280
And one of the most standard things they'll say

53
01:82:11,280 --> 01:82:15,200
is if you cut workers' nominal base pay, it hurts morale.

54
01:82:16,000 --> 01:82:18,280
All right, and again, well, why,

55
01:82:18,280 --> 01:82:21,240
well, if they cut their, if they don't work as hard,

56
01:82:21,240 --> 01:82:23,000
I'll just cut their pay again.

57
01:82:23,000 --> 01:82:25,160
Yeah, but they'll cut their productivity again,

58
01:82:25,160 --> 01:82:26,000
and there will be the spiral.

59
01:82:26,000 --> 01:82:27,720
And here's the key thing.

60
01:82:27,720 --> 01:82:30,400
When you go and cut workers' pay,

61
01:82:30,400 --> 01:82:33,000
what is obvious is that your workers,

62
01:82:33,000 --> 01:82:35,280
on average, are less productive.

63
01:82:35,280 --> 01:82:36,880
What is generally not so obvious

64
01:82:36,880 --> 01:82:39,380
is which workers are slacking.

65
01:82:41,080 --> 01:82:43,960
So again, the story is not that the individual worker

66
01:82:43,960 --> 01:82:45,880
says, hey, you know how you cut my pay?

67
01:82:45,880 --> 01:82:47,800
Well, I'm gonna retaliate by not working hard

68
01:82:47,800 --> 01:82:49,160
during the recession.

69
01:82:49,160 --> 01:82:51,940
That's a pretty good way to get fired right away.

70
01:82:51,940 --> 01:82:55,620
Instead, what workers usually do is silently,

71
01:82:55,620 --> 01:82:56,960
oh, come on, man, I just got fired.

72
01:82:56,960 --> 01:82:59,640
I'm gonna get my wages and go from there.

73
01:82:59,640 --> 01:83:01,440
And there's a lot of people like this,

74
01:83:01,440 --> 01:83:03,960
all of them going and trying to,

75
01:83:05,200 --> 01:83:07,400
at minimum, they just don't wanna give you

76
01:83:07,400 --> 01:83:09,160
anything extra anymore.

77
01:83:09,160 --> 01:83:11,120
And a lot of them actually are looking for some way

78
01:83:11,120 --> 01:83:13,080
to get revenge, to get back at the man.

79
01:83:14,120 --> 01:83:16,400
All right, so the real problem then

80
01:83:16,400 --> 01:83:18,160
is an employer goes and cuts pay by 20%,

81
01:83:18,160 --> 01:83:19,840
and the productivity of the workforce

82
01:83:19,840 --> 01:83:22,560
goes and falls by, say, 25%.

83
01:83:22,560 --> 01:83:24,320
And then if you say, well, just fire the workers

84
01:83:24,320 --> 01:83:27,200
who reduced the productivity,

85
01:83:27,200 --> 01:83:28,040
that's like, well, I don't know.

86
01:83:28,040 --> 01:83:30,640
It just seemed like the whole group turned against me.

87
01:83:31,820 --> 01:83:33,120
And if that is your situation,

88
01:83:33,120 --> 01:83:35,440
there's a reason to not cut the pay of the group.

89
01:83:36,880 --> 01:83:38,560
And again, what really upsets people, of course,

90
01:83:38,560 --> 01:83:40,320
is if you go and fire some people

91
01:83:40,320 --> 01:83:41,960
and then replace them with some other cheaper,

92
01:83:41,960 --> 01:83:43,720
where there's some cheaper replacements,

93
01:83:43,720 --> 01:83:44,800
because the people who are fired

94
01:83:44,800 --> 01:83:47,960
are generally the friends of the other workers.

95
01:83:47,960 --> 01:83:50,160
Now, why not go and fire the entire workforce

96
01:83:50,160 --> 01:83:51,720
and replace it with new people?

97
01:83:52,680 --> 01:83:54,160
The main problem here seems to be

98
01:83:54,160 --> 01:83:56,580
that the way people learn jobs is by practice,

99
01:83:56,580 --> 01:83:58,320
and the best kind of practice is practice

100
01:83:58,320 --> 01:84:01,440
under the tutelage of someone who already does that job.

101
01:84:01,440 --> 01:84:03,840
So it's not safe to go and fire

102
01:84:03,840 --> 01:84:05,660
the entire workforce generally.

103
01:84:05,660 --> 01:84:08,920
Rupert Murdoch actually famously did this.

104
01:84:08,920 --> 01:84:12,280
Rupert Murdoch actually went and,

105
01:84:12,280 --> 01:84:14,720
he was sick of dealing with the printers' unions,

106
01:84:14,720 --> 01:84:19,520
so he surreptitiously went and created totally new factories

107
01:84:19,520 --> 01:84:22,460
like hundreds of miles from the existing ones

108
01:84:22,460 --> 01:84:24,760
and hired a totally new workforce,

109
01:84:24,760 --> 01:84:29,200
and then he provoked the existing ones into striking,

110
01:84:29,200 --> 01:84:30,440
and then he just shut the factories,

111
01:84:30,440 --> 01:84:31,780
and that was the end of it.

112
01:84:31,780 --> 01:84:33,760
And he said, yeah, and like, we'll strike.

113
01:84:33,760 --> 01:84:36,120
Well, you strike at the closed factories,

114
01:84:36,120 --> 01:84:37,200
and no one here anymore,

115
01:84:37,200 --> 01:84:38,480
nothing, I'm never gonna be open again,

116
01:84:38,480 --> 01:84:40,120
so strike there all you want,

117
01:84:40,120 --> 01:84:42,360
but fine, then we'll go strike at the new ones.

118
01:84:42,360 --> 01:84:45,240
If you wanna go and move 500 miles away from your home,

119
01:84:45,240 --> 01:84:47,760
I deliberately found a place as far away in England

120
01:84:47,760 --> 01:84:51,040
as one can get from your current English location.

121
01:84:51,040 --> 01:84:53,240
All right, you know the size of England?

122
01:84:53,240 --> 01:84:55,360
That's the distance between where you are

123
01:84:55,360 --> 01:84:58,080
and where you'll have to go to strike, right?

124
01:84:58,080 --> 01:85:01,400
And that's where very few people were that committed

125
01:85:01,400 --> 01:85:04,500
to go and move 500 miles away to go and strike.

126
01:85:05,320 --> 01:85:06,160
Yes?

127
01:85:06,160 --> 01:85:06,980
Didn't Truman Buley find that,

128
01:85:06,980 --> 01:85:09,040
like, in a very small percentage of the cases,

129
01:85:09,040 --> 01:85:11,440
the employer, like, goes in front of the whole workforce

130
01:85:11,440 --> 01:85:13,120
and says, look, I have to cut nominal wages

131
01:85:13,120 --> 01:85:15,080
or else I'll have to fire some of you,

132
01:85:15,080 --> 01:85:17,480
and in that case, the productivity didn't shrink?

133
01:85:17,480 --> 01:85:18,540
Well, I think it was stronger.

134
01:85:18,540 --> 01:85:20,280
I think it was, I have to do it,

135
01:85:20,280 --> 01:85:22,920
or like, because we all have to say we're losing money.

136
01:85:22,920 --> 01:85:23,760
Oh, okay.

137
01:85:23,760 --> 01:85:24,800
So I think, so, you know, like,

138
01:85:24,800 --> 01:85:26,440
we found is, yes, workers are more flexible

139
01:85:26,440 --> 01:85:28,840
when you actually say you are losing money.

140
01:85:28,840 --> 01:85:30,560
Is there a reason that hasn't caught on more

141
01:85:30,560 --> 01:85:31,400
during the session?

142
01:85:31,400 --> 01:85:32,760
Yes.

143
01:85:32,760 --> 01:85:33,600
Well, of course, a lot of firms,

144
01:85:33,600 --> 01:85:35,000
like, most firms are still making money.

145
01:85:35,000 --> 01:85:36,200
